WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.600
The day's been running on time and we're going to try to do

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:10.259
the same. My name is Paul Yak. I'd like to welcome you and

00:00:10.279 --> 00:00:14.629
this is the last spring in the afternoon. So, we've got a

00:00:14.649 --> 00:00:19.469
great panel put together. We're going to focus on Med Tech.

00:00:19.489 --> 00:00:25.050
And as you can tell we have some innovators who are of a

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:30.449
vintage that they're out there doing this in the first years

00:00:30.469 --> 00:00:35.479
after launching from Stanford. And what we want to hear is

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.159
kind of their perspective. Things are changing so radically

00:00:38.179 --> 00:00:41.309
as you all know, as we've been hearing about today. And we'

00:00:41.309 --> 00:00:45.529
re going to get some in the trenches perspectives about

00:00:45.549 --> 00:00:46.879
what's going on.

00:00:46.899 --> 00:00:53.339
The bios of our panelists are in your programs, but I want

00:00:53.359 --> 00:00:56.609
to just briefly introduce each of them. So, on the far left

00:00:56.629 --> 00:01:00.609
is Darren Hide who's a principal from

00:01:00.629 --> 00:01:04.040
[inaudible] Darren has an MBA from Stanford, his AB from

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:09.430
Princeton, did medical school training, worked for Med

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:12.299
Tronic for a number of years.

00:01:12.319 --> 00:01:14.450
Next to him is Moe

00:01:14.469 --> 00:01:18.049
[inaudible] who's recently taken a new job as Executive vice

00:01:18.069 --> 00:01:22.870
President/Chief Strategy Officer for West Wireless

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:26.370
Technologies. He'll tell us about that, but a new initiative

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:31.540
down in San Diego focusing on technology advance and cost

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.370
savings through wireless technologies.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:36.269
Next to him is Darren

00:01:36.289 --> 00:01:39.230
[inaudible] who's CEO and Founder of Hourglass Technologies

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:45.120
Company in the obesity space. Darren's an Engineer Economist

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:48.980
from Duke who also got his MBA from Stanford.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:55.299
And to my left is Udai Kumar. Udai did his undergraduate and

00:01:55.319 --> 00:02:00.039
his M.D. at Harvard, residency at Columbia, Cardiology

00:02:00.059 --> 00:02:03.700
Fellowship at UCSF, was a Bio Design fellow and he founded

00:02:03.719 --> 00:02:07.230
Eye Rhythm Technologies where he is still the Chief Medical

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:10.679
Officer. He's also active with the Bio Design Program as a

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:12.579
Global Fellowship Director.

00:02:12.599 --> 00:02:20.209
So, we would really love to have your questions and input.

00:02:20.229 --> 00:02:25.049
What I'd like to do to kind of prime the pump is to find out

00:02:25.069 --> 00:02:31.449
from each of these folks kind of the one key thing-the most

00:02:31.469 --> 00:02:33.739
important thing they've experienced that they didn't

00:02:33.759 --> 00:02:40.519
anticipate at all coming out of their training program. And

00:02:40.539 --> 00:02:43.389
they may need to tell us a little bit more about the context

00:02:43.389 --> 00:02:48.379
of their company to answer this. But Udai, let me start with

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.709
you and we're going to go rapid fire. So, your

00:02:51.729 --> 00:02:55.319
responsibility is to set the pace of going quickly with this

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:56.340
answer.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:59.979
Sure. Thanks, Paul. Thanks to everyone for staying through

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.199
the afternoon to hear us. So, I think I'll just give you a

00:03:04.219 --> 00:03:07.169
brief background on the company so you know the space. I'm a

00:03:07.169 --> 00:03:09.489
cardiologist/cardio physiologist by training. So, I deal

00:03:09.509 --> 00:03:13.309
with abnormal heart rhythms. As a by design fellow we focus

00:03:13.329 --> 00:03:16.689
on the field of electrophysiology and arrhythmia care to try

00:03:16.709 --> 00:03:18.449
to understand what are met needs.

00:03:18.469 --> 00:03:21.379
Long story short, we realized that there was an unmet need

00:03:21.399 --> 00:03:24.259
in diagnosing people not by specialists, but at the primary

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:26.569
care/emergency room level. You've heard a lot about that

00:03:26.569 --> 00:03:29.069
today. We could have anticipated it, but we were thinking

00:03:29.069 --> 00:03:33.789
ACO models even before there was one. But the long and short

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:39.149
of it is that we started the idea of a simple, single use

00:03:39.169 --> 00:03:42.669
long-term disposable cardiac monitor that could be placed in

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:45.749
those settings so that with one test the patient could get

00:03:45.769 --> 00:03:47.919
diagnosed. And one of our requirements was really driving

00:03:47.919 --> 00:03:51.509
the cost down so it was really at the cost of a blood test.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:54.999
So, I decided to take the company forward after I finished

00:03:55.019 --> 00:03:57.899
the Bio Design fellowship, really spent a lot of time

00:03:57.919 --> 00:03:59.629
learning about what it means to be able to articulate a

00:03:59.629 --> 00:04:00.629
value

00:04:00.629 --> 00:04:03.809
Proposition and really understand why this need. I really

00:04:03.829 --> 00:04:07.259
wanted to have the idea killed if I could because I was

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:11.439
giving up being a practicing physician, so I really wanted

00:04:11.459 --> 00:04:14.459
to see that this was going to go somewhere. And you'll see

00:04:14.459 --> 00:04:17.709
at the end of what I'm going to say why all these elements

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:18.720
are important.

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:23.500
Four years now fast forward, we're 130 employees. We're

00:04:23.519 --> 00:04:26.629
selling our product widely across the U.S. We should hit our

00:04:26.649 --> 00:04:29.269
10,000th device at the end of this month and hopefully do

00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:33.600
20,000-30,000 this year. And what we're really seeing is

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:36.530
Todd and other people that you've heard today have really

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:40.040
seen what's a different type of model in terms of trying to

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:42.959
focus on not just the unmet need, but unmet need in a

00:04:42.959 --> 00:04:45.350
business innovation/business model sense.

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:48.360
We realized that specialists oftentimes see patients they

00:04:48.379 --> 00:04:51.590
don't need to see and oftentimes getting to a specialist is

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:55.040
a problem. Really focusing on getting devices down to

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:58.069
primary care level, but understanding how primary care and

00:04:58.089 --> 00:05:01.519
emergency room physicians practice and the cost constraints

00:05:01.539 --> 00:05:05.899
on the healthcare system has really, really been important

00:05:05.919 --> 00:05:08.800
in the sense that when we're down in the low $100 range for

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:11.889
a device that records for a long period of time that's easy

00:05:11.889 --> 00:05:14.889
for patients to use we use cloud computing to do all our

00:05:14.889 --> 00:05:17.959
algorithm analysis so we're really scalable. And more

00:05:17.979 --> 00:05:19.579
importantly, from the government standpoint we've done this

00:05:19.579 --> 00:05:23.009
all in the U.S. So, we've created 130 U.S. jobs. When you

00:05:23.009 --> 00:05:25.759
put all that together you can see that value and innovation

00:05:25.759 --> 00:05:29.180
don't necessarily go, are not apathetical to one another.

00:05:29.199 --> 00:05:32.170
They can actually survive, coexist and represent solutions

00:05:32.189 --> 00:05:33.370
to good opportunities.

00:05:33.389 --> 00:05:36.250
So, that's just a high level background and I left a lot of

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:39.250
things out in the interest of time. But three things I think

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:41.500
are important for anybody thinking about starting a company.

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:42.689
1. Know your limitations.

00:05:42.709 --> 00:05:45.819
Know what you're good at and what you're not good at. I'm a

00:05:45.819 --> 00:05:48.319
physician. I think I'm reasonably smart, but I don't know

00:05:48.319 --> 00:05:51.069
everything. Putting CEO after my name would make no sense to

00:05:51.069 --> 00:05:53.500
an investor. Understanding the unmet need, understanding

00:05:53.519 --> 00:05:55.300
clinical, understanding how to develop something from a

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:57.959
research standpoint and clinical standpoint? Yes. So, when I

00:05:57.979 --> 00:06:00.669
started out one of the first things I did was try and

00:06:00.669 --> 00:06:03.979
understand, "This is what I can do, this is what I can't do.

00:06:03.979 --> 00:06:06.729
But can I attract people that can fill in those gaps?

00:06:06.729 --> 00:06:10.620
2. Understand the risk of your project.

00:06:10.639 --> 00:06:13.479
And a lot of fellows have heard me say this. People always

00:06:13.479 --> 00:06:15.479
think that developing a prototype's the most important.

00:06:15.479 --> 00:06:18.229
Sometimes it is, sometimes it's not. In my case, I don't

00:06:18.229 --> 00:06:20.729
think anybody didn't think we could develop the device. It

00:06:20.729 --> 00:06:23.229
was really how do you change a paradigm of delivering

00:06:23.229 --> 00:06:25.229
healthcare to the U.S. That's business execution.

00:06:25.229 --> 00:06:28.540
So, if you understand the limitations and you understand

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:31.799
what the risk is, my risk was, "How do you secure some IP?"

00:06:31.799 --> 00:06:33.649
And more importantly to protect what we're going to do. But

00:06:33.649 --> 00:06:36.139
also more importantly, "How do I attract someone with

00:06:36.159 --> 00:06:39.839
industry experience who can help fill in that void?" So, my

00:06:39.859 --> 00:06:42.689
first hire or the person who came on board when I started

00:06:42.689 --> 00:06:45.689
the company is the CEO who's still the CEO, who had multiple

00:06:45.689 --> 00:06:47.689
years experience in cardiac rhythm monitoring at different

00:06:47.689 --> 00:06:49.220
companies, so understood that landscape.

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:51.819
3. Understand macroeconomic trends.

00:06:51.839 --> 00:06:55.810
You've heard it around all day today about the issues of

00:06:55.829 --> 00:06:58.829
business model innovation that value's now going to become a

00:06:58.849 --> 00:07:01.549
requirement. It's not just nice to have. It's not just

00:07:01.549 --> 00:07:04.509
develop a new technology and then later on figure out how to

00:07:04.509 --> 00:07:07.259
pay for it. You actually have to think about how you're

00:07:07.259 --> 00:07:09.509
going to get paid for it from the beginning.

00:07:09.509 --> 00:07:12.259
So, we didn't talk about the regulatory risk because in our

00:07:12.259 --> 00:07:14.759
case it wasn't as big a risk. But understanding these

00:07:14.759 --> 00:07:17.610
factors of where healthcare was going, obviously when Obama

00:07:17.629 --> 00:07:19.719
was elected we couldn't have predicted the Affordable Care

00:07:19.719 --> 00:07:22.430
Act. But there were trends already going in that direction.

00:07:22.449 --> 00:07:25.699
So, having as a requirement lowering the cost of healthcare

00:07:25.719 --> 00:07:28.730
and fundamentally solving the need was really important.

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:31.579
So, if you take those things-understand the big picture so

00:07:31.599 --> 00:07:34.410
that your need really is going to be supportable by what

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:37.189
investors are looking at, understand your risks so you know

00:07:37.209 --> 00:07:39.339
what things you can address and what you can't, and then

00:07:39.339 --> 00:07:42.000
understanding yourself to understand, "Can you meet those

00:07:42.019 --> 00:07:45.290
challenges?" And if they don't all align it doesn't mean it'

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:48.810
s not a good idea, it doesn't mean it's not a good deed. It

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:51.810
just may not be the right time for you to solve that

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:53.810
problem. So, those are the kinds of things I've learned so

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:54.810
far.

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:57.810
That's great, Udai. And I'm going to ask you to keep track

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:00.560
if you have a question for the specific speaker. We're going

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:03.060
to go through these kind of preliminary comments first and

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:04.060
then open it up.

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:07.240
And Darren, in some sense it must make you cringe to hear

00:08:07.259 --> 00:08:11.899
that regulatory is not a particular issue from Udai. But

00:08:11.919 --> 00:08:12.919
tell us about...

00:08:12.919 --> 00:08:14.419
We have other solutions for that.

00:08:13.669 --> 00:08:14.669
Yeah.

00:08:14.669 --> 00:08:19.339
So, Hourglass Technologies, we're developing a medical

00:08:19.359 --> 00:08:23.230
device in the obesity space. Currently, the only effective

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:28.290
treatments for patients who are morbidly obese-over 100 lbs

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:30.269
overweight-are invasive surgeries like gastric bypass and

00:08:30.269 --> 00:08:34.529
gastric banding surgeries. They're very effective, but of

00:08:34.549 --> 00:08:38.340
course they're invasive. They take place in an expensive

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:42.879
operating room and they carry multiple complications.

00:08:42.899 --> 00:08:48.169
So, what we're doing at Hourglass Technologies is we're

00:08:48.189 --> 00:08:52.559
making these procedures much less invasive. We've converted

00:08:52.579 --> 00:08:56.519
them to a fully Trans oral procedure so there are absolutely

00:08:56.539 --> 00:09:01.870
no incisions. So, it's an incision free procedure that will

00:09:01.889 --> 00:09:06.070
dramatically reduce the complication rates and also make it

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:09.850
a much more cost effective procedure which we've heard

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:14.269
throughout the day is very much on the minds of every

00:09:14.289 --> 00:09:19.069
country in the world and it's coming to the U.S. as well.

00:09:19.089 --> 00:09:24.610
So, just a little bit about the company itself. We're a 15

00:09:24.629 --> 00:09:29.669
person company. As Paul was eluding to, this is a Class 3

00:09:29.689 --> 00:09:38.889
PMA change the world kind of device and that brings up a lot

00:09:38.909 --> 00:09:42.919
of regulatory hurdles along the way. We've raised $11

00:09:42.939 --> 00:09:46.909
million to date and we've been able to partner with great

00:09:46.929 --> 00:09:51.179
partners including Johnson &amp; Johnson, Maverick Capital, and

00:09:51.199 --> 00:09:57.129
Exceed Capital. And that will kind of tie into kind of my

00:09:57.149 --> 00:09:59.899
learning's along the way.

00:09:59.919 --> 00:10:04.129
So, a big learning for me early on was I had no idea how

00:10:04.149 --> 00:10:08.889
difficult it was going to be to raise funding. I thought, "

00:10:08.909 --> 00:10:12.870
Here you are-obesity. You can look at the cover of Time

00:10:12.889 --> 00:10:17.179
Magazine. You can look anywhere. It's a really big issue in

00:10:17.199 --> 00:10:22.059
healthcare." Now, it was an extremely crowded space before I

00:10:22.079 --> 00:10:23.149
walked into it.

00:10:23.169 --> 00:10:25.220
So, there are lots of great entrepreneurs and great

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:30.009
companies working there. And what Udai eluded to, you have

00:10:30.029 --> 00:10:34.370
to understand the risk that you're getting into. And for us

00:10:34.389 --> 00:10:38.309
what we realized is we needed to really separate the risks

00:10:38.329 --> 00:10:42.379
out. And it wasn't about, okay, just getting a device that

00:10:42.399 --> 00:10:46.409
would work in patients. It was going much more fundamental

00:10:46.429 --> 00:10:48.970
and saying, "Well, what are the issues that people are

00:10:48.990 --> 00:10:53.120
having?" And it was the biology. It was the fact that the

00:10:53.139 --> 00:10:56.389
stomach is an incredible organ, but it's very difficult to

00:10:56.409 --> 00:10:58.909
actually control the stomach.

00:10:58.929 --> 00:11:03.399
And so, we were able to split our whole system into parts

00:11:03.419 --> 00:11:06.259
and instead of coming up with a full-fledged prototype we

00:11:06.279 --> 00:11:10.789
were able to literally build a small device for $10 on my

00:11:10.809 --> 00:11:15.259
kitchen table, and implant that in some pre-clinical models,

00:11:15.279 --> 00:11:19.909
and show that, "Look, this has a lot of promise" and use

00:11:19.929 --> 00:11:23.500
that as the leverage point to raise the capital to move the

00:11:23.519 --> 00:11:29.559
company forward. But it really took division of risk to be

00:11:29.579 --> 00:11:32.389
able to move the needle with investors.

00:11:32.409 --> 00:11:37.689
Another area that I never anticipated was the type of

00:11:37.709 --> 00:11:44.149
funding sources. Obesity and because it's a Class 3 PMA

00:11:44.169 --> 00:11:47.059
device it's going to take a lot of capital to get this

00:11:47.059 --> 00:11:52.149
project to market. Some of our competitors have spent almost

00:11:52.169 --> 00:11:56.620
$90 million and still never quite made it to market. And so,

00:11:56.639 --> 00:12:00.529
it's forced us to look at alternative funding sources. So,

00:12:00.549 --> 00:12:04.470
rather than going a very traditional VC route, we've

00:12:04.490 --> 00:12:08.949
partnered with strategic's, with hedge funds, and really had

00:12:08.969 --> 00:12:13.009
to look at this in a very different way much because

00:12:13.029 --> 00:12:16.740
traditional venture has been shying away from really early

00:12:16.759 --> 00:12:21.860
stage, pre-clinical, Class 3, high regulatory class devices.

00:12:21.879 --> 00:12:27.490
So, just keeping the company alive to make sure we can help

00:12:27.509 --> 00:12:32.460
treat our first patients has certainly been an eye opening

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:36.529
experience. But it's been a very rewarding experience and we

00:12:36.549 --> 00:12:38.659
can't wait to treat our first patient here very soon.

00:12:38.659 --> 00:12:41.939
Sort of an interesting juxtaposition here of two different

00:12:41.959 --> 00:12:48.120
models. Something where you wound up Udai, in really a cost

00:12:48.139 --> 00:12:52.939
saving, a little bit of a new space and aligned with what

00:12:52.959 --> 00:12:56.799
we're hearing about today. Darren, in some sense you're a

00:12:56.819 --> 00:13:00.579
classic strategy of taking an invasive procedure and making

00:13:00.599 --> 00:13:03.899
it less invasive-in your case considerably less invasive.

00:13:03.919 --> 00:13:07.840
But different funding strategies. You had to invent your

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:10.259
different funding strategies and I suspect we'll want to

00:13:10.259 --> 00:13:12.009
circle back on that in a minute.

00:13:12.009 --> 00:13:17.659
But Moe, you're coming at this from a really different

00:13:17.679 --> 00:13:21.110
perspective. And Moe, I didn't mention-you'll see in the

00:13:21.129 --> 00:13:27.500
bio-spent time at the FCC looking at kind of macro issues of

00:13:27.519 --> 00:13:31.579
cost, and connectedness, and value. So, tell us where you're

00:13:31.599 --> 00:13:32.599
at and how you got there.

00:13:32.599 --> 00:13:40.110
Sure. So, M.D. background, then dabbled a bit in venture

00:13:40.129 --> 00:13:43.960
capital devices, IT services, as well as biotech, and policy

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:47.100
background way back in the day, and then decided to jump on

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:49.620
the whole reform wagon and build the first healthcare team

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:51.370
of the FCC. And I think Tom

00:13:51.370 --> 00:13:54.009
[inaudible] summed it up very well this morning that health

00:13:54.029 --> 00:13:56.779
reform's a lot more than just adding 30 million more people

00:13:56.779 --> 00:13:59.529
in the system. It's actually how do we change the delivery

00:13:59.529 --> 00:14:01.279
mechanism, the incentive mechanism, and then how does

00:14:01.279 --> 00:14:03.139
technology empower this?

00:14:03.159 --> 00:14:04.480
And then I ended up at the

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:05.980
[inaudible] Health Institute. We're essentially a startup-an

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:09.929
18 month old institute based in LaHoya. We have a couple of

00:14:09.949 --> 00:14:13.329
different functions. We have an investment fund. We have now

00:14:13.349 --> 00:14:16.049
around 25 engineers. We incubate technologies, we spin them

00:14:16.069 --> 00:14:19.819
out, and we do a range of policy issues as well. And they're

00:14:19.839 --> 00:14:22.339
all around the cost reduction thesis, so they're not too

00:14:22.339 --> 00:14:24.589
dissimilar to some of the things Udai was saying.

00:14:24.589 --> 00:14:27.589
And I was asked to talk about some of my shared experiences

00:14:27.589 --> 00:14:31.189
or learning's. I think the main thing I've learned, I've had

00:14:31.209 --> 00:14:34.129
most success in my career when I focused on-and I've been

00:14:34.129 --> 00:14:36.240
lucky in some respects focusing on-high growth areas and

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:39.379
being an early person within that space. And I'll give you

00:14:39.399 --> 00:14:40.679
some stories around that.

00:14:40.699 --> 00:14:42.839
At one point I was looking at opportunities around medical

00:14:42.839 --> 00:14:46.679
device startups. Although I haven't practiced in a long time

00:14:46.699 --> 00:14:48.990
clinically and I'd done business school-I had business

00:14:48.990 --> 00:14:52.220
experience-I was told I was still a clinical person and had

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:55.899
to focus on junior clinical roles. My friend in the front

00:14:55.919 --> 00:14:57.230
row is laughing at me.

00:14:57.250 --> 00:15:00.709
And then the week after I was interviewing in an executive

00:15:00.729 --> 00:15:03.879
position within an online healthcare delivery platform. So,

00:15:03.899 --> 00:15:07.750
I think the law of supply and demand also apply to how you

00:15:07.769 --> 00:15:09.860
think about your career.

00:15:09.879 --> 00:15:12.629
The first guys that worked for Google there was no internet

00:15:12.629 --> 00:15:15.529
company for you to get experience in. Medical devices bio

00:15:15.549 --> 00:15:18.850
pharm to an extent is a 20 year old industry. So, the skill

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:21.579
sets and the experiences always increase as time goes on.

00:15:21.599 --> 00:15:25.620
First M.D.'s working at Genentech had just an M.D., now it's

00:15:25.639 --> 00:15:29.059
an M.D., Ph.D., residency. So, I think that's the thing I've

00:15:29.059 --> 00:15:31.809
learned the most. By trying to pick high growth areas and

00:15:31.809 --> 00:15:34.809
being an early person to help shape it, has helped me in

00:15:34.809 --> 00:15:36.929
better stead.

00:15:36.949 --> 00:15:40.840
Darren, so you're responsible for funding all of these

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:44.730
people out here. So, your perspectives?

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:48.720
Yeah. So, I work at Aberdeen Adventures fresh out of Dr.

00:15:48.740 --> 00:15:54.569
Yak's Bio Design class and the GSB here. We do early stage

00:15:54.589 --> 00:15:58.589
device investing, biotech investing, wireless health

00:15:58.609 --> 00:16:02.259
investing hopefully here in the near future.

00:16:02.279 --> 00:16:07.970
And I would just say that in my five years already I feel

00:16:07.990 --> 00:16:10.849
like I've seen a lot because things were working great when

00:16:10.849 --> 00:16:15.289
I came in 2006. Everything's on the path. Your product's

00:16:15.309 --> 00:16:19.990
working, you're hiring good people, things are looking great

00:16:20.009 --> 00:16:22.759
and then all the sudden things that are completely out of

00:16:22.759 --> 00:16:25.259
your control when you make the investment might have a

00:16:25.259 --> 00:16:27.100
really big impact on how things go.

00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:30.250
So, the thing I learned the most is you can do all the

00:16:30.250 --> 00:16:33.629
upfront work, and figure out where the risks are going to

00:16:33.649 --> 00:16:36.399
be, and vet everything you can. That might not have really

00:16:36.399 --> 00:16:39.879
that much of an impact on what happens down the line.

00:16:39.899 --> 00:16:44.329
So, what we've tried to do is stay out in front like Moe

00:16:44.349 --> 00:16:46.649
said and I think that area he's landed in is really

00:16:46.649 --> 00:16:50.610
interesting. And then find great people like Darren and Udai

00:16:50.629 --> 00:16:54.730
down here who are able to be nimble in difficult times and

00:16:54.750 --> 00:16:58.769
be resourceful like Darren has been in difficult times that

00:16:58.789 --> 00:17:01.799
find their way to success even when things are harder than

00:17:01.819 --> 00:17:04.660
they might have seemed at the beginning.

00:17:04.680 --> 00:17:09.450
So, let's do start getting questions and let me focus you

00:17:09.470 --> 00:17:14.190
too. I see a fair number of young people-if I may say so-in

00:17:14.210 --> 00:17:17.859
the audience and if you have career oriented questions-the

00:17:17.879 --> 00:17:20.490
other things we're going to go on and talk about. I'm really

00:17:20.490 --> 00:17:23.769
curious to hear what each of these folks is doing with a

00:17:23.789 --> 00:17:28.879
global strategy. I want to get to that. And then in the

00:17:28.899 --> 00:17:32.420
spirit of the day from each of their perspectives kind of

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:35.839
where things are going, how they're seeing around the

00:17:35.859 --> 00:17:36.859
corners.

00:17:36.859 --> 00:17:51.799
But anything career wise,

00:17:51.819 --> 00:17:56.170
[Informal Talk] So, questions-Moe, I'm going to pick on you,

00:17:56.190 --> 00:18:01.309
okay? I'm going to ask you if you wouldn't mind going to the

00:18:01.329 --> 00:18:05.779
mike. So, Moe as many of you know also could well be up here

00:18:05.799 --> 00:18:13.589
as one of the Founder/CEO's of a really rocking company. But

00:18:13.609 --> 00:18:17.819
if we put you on the spot, what didn't you know about

00:18:17.839 --> 00:18:22.389
getting into the career when you were here as an MBA? What

00:18:22.409 --> 00:18:26.049
kind of piece of advice would you give?

00:18:26.069 --> 00:18:30.399
I guess the area with regards to which I was most na?ve is

00:18:30.419 --> 00:18:34.000
that I thought with respect to all the government

00:18:34.019 --> 00:18:38.129
interaction is being right and making sense would be

00:18:38.149 --> 00:18:42.680
sufficient. So, our value proposition was quite similar to

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:46.250
that. We basically looked at something which was one of the

00:18:46.269 --> 00:18:50.930
largest VME or medical equipment spent items for the agency

00:18:50.950 --> 00:18:55.819
and said we could eliminate almost half of this cost

00:18:55.839 --> 00:19:00.440
immediately. And we thought that would suffice. And three

00:19:00.460 --> 00:19:07.470
years later it's clearly not sufficient and that's an area

00:19:07.490 --> 00:19:11.670
where I feel especially someone early on needs to enhance

00:19:11.690 --> 00:19:16.930
its understanding on what it would really take to galvanize

00:19:16.950 --> 00:19:21.980
a government agency into an action. Even if the action is

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:25.509
beneficial to it, it often requires more reasoning or

00:19:25.529 --> 00:19:26.529
rationale.

00:19:26.529 --> 00:19:27.529
Yeah, Udai?

00:19:27.529 --> 00:19:32.029
Again, I was just going to say I think one of the things Moe

00:19:32.049 --> 00:19:35.299
just hit on is an important thing to think about in terms of

00:19:35.299 --> 00:19:38.549
when you lay out your plan. Is the cost it really takes to

00:19:38.549 --> 00:19:41.049
understand reimbursement having both he and I here on the

00:19:41.049 --> 00:19:44.049
same page in terms of one of the biggest problems with the

00:19:44.049 --> 00:19:46.589
healthcare system innovation today is that you can go

00:19:46.609 --> 00:19:49.309
through the entire FDA process and have a device that's

00:19:49.309 --> 00:19:53.379
deemed safe by the U.S. government. But payers-particularly

00:19:53.399 --> 00:19:56.849
CMS and then private payers-will wait for you to underwrite

00:19:56.869 --> 00:20:00.869
usage of a device, clinically show adoption, show trials to

00:20:00.889 --> 00:20:04.139
then pay you even though at the end of the day particularly-

00:20:04.159 --> 00:20:07.559
again in my case-if you take into account the value that

00:20:07.579 --> 00:20:11.139
could benefit them it's not a justifiable marketplace at all

00:20:11.159 --> 00:20:12.409
and there's no risk sharing.

00:20:12.409 --> 00:20:15.659
So, I think one of the other things you asked, Paul about a

00:20:15.659 --> 00:20:18.659
global sense of what we do on the India and Singapore side-

00:20:18.659 --> 00:20:23.609
it's clear that today other countries can develop products

00:20:23.629 --> 00:20:26.559
just as good as ones that have been developed here. But

00:20:26.579 --> 00:20:30.440
they've been doing so with an eye towards lower cost. So, at

00:20:30.460 --> 00:20:34.269
some point healthcare payers or healthcare entities, if I

00:20:34.289 --> 00:20:37.200
have a stint from India that's just as good, and it's just

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:39.019
as safe, and it's a stint from

00:20:39.019 --> 00:20:41.960
[inaudible] Scientific or Medtronic here I have to make and

00:20:41.980 --> 00:20:46.180
one's half the price but effective I know which one I'm

00:20:46.200 --> 00:20:48.200
going to pick because it's a rational decision.

00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:50.950
So, if the U.S. government wants to think about innovating

00:20:50.950 --> 00:20:53.950
jobs they have to address questions that both Moe and I have

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:57.539
provided them. How do you risk share with us? I mean, if we

00:20:57.559 --> 00:21:00.079
meet milestones just like VC's want us to meet milestones

00:21:00.079 --> 00:21:03.039
for regulatory, why can't there be reimbursement milestones

00:21:03.059 --> 00:21:06.049
that we'll prove to you in the next year we'll cost share

00:21:06.049 --> 00:21:09.799
for this. If you hit it we pay some more, you hit it we pay

00:21:09.799 --> 00:21:12.799
some more until some are agreed upon and if we don't we

00:21:12.799 --> 00:21:16.240
haven't proved that we're of superior outcome. Maybe we don'

00:21:16.259 --> 00:21:17.990
t deserve to be in the marketplace.

00:21:17.990 --> 00:21:21.079
So, that's something that companies will have to do. Do they

00:21:21.099 --> 00:21:24.349
really want to do that? And it goes back to the kind of

00:21:24.349 --> 00:21:28.099
things that Dr. Manuel said yesterday. If you really have a

00:21:28.119 --> 00:21:30.869
much better technology don't worry about it and the payment

00:21:30.869 --> 00:21:33.369
will come if you really are going to deliver better

00:21:33.369 --> 00:21:35.869
outcomes. But I think this whole issue of innovative small

00:21:35.869 --> 00:21:38.480
companies raising tens and tens of millions of dollars after

00:21:38.500 --> 00:21:42.480
developing a working product that's regulated and cleared is

00:21:42.500 --> 00:21:46.919
somewhat crazy and is definitely an issue with the spurring

00:21:46.939 --> 00:21:49.599
innovation. You need to think about that because that's

00:21:49.619 --> 00:21:52.369
still going to be a reality for the next few years.

00:21:52.369 --> 00:21:56.529
So, let's try to drill down in this a little bit. Ted Park

00:21:56.549 --> 00:21:59.299
in one of the lunch breakout sessions was saying, "Look, it'

00:21:59.299 --> 00:22:02.220
s clear you have to go to the emerging accountable care

00:22:02.240 --> 00:22:06.589
organizations and sell your new technology to them, okay?

00:22:06.609 --> 00:22:12.190
So, you're out there trying to do this. Is there any there

00:22:12.210 --> 00:22:13.559
to go to?

00:22:13.579 --> 00:22:19.259
Surprisingly big payers today-United and others-are seeing

00:22:19.279 --> 00:22:21.990
that they need to embrace it. So, while there are still some

00:22:21.990 --> 00:22:24.879
conservative private payers who don't recognize the value a

00:22:24.899 --> 00:22:28.149
lot of other ones are trying to take a look, and trying to

00:22:28.149 --> 00:22:31.149
look at data, and trying to figure out how do we promote

00:22:31.149 --> 00:22:33.649
this to our beneficiaries. But again, we're at the front

00:22:33.649 --> 00:22:35.990
edge of this and what really happens-and again, you saw

00:22:35.990 --> 00:22:40.119
Todd. He's as enthusiastic as you'll get except for

00:22:40.139 --> 00:22:42.700
[inaudible] Those two guys are really trying to figure out

00:22:42.720 --> 00:22:44.659
ways of doing something.

00:22:44.679 --> 00:22:47.029
But again, they're working with the government and one thing

00:22:47.029 --> 00:22:50.529
I like saying is the speed of a startup is not the speed of

00:22:50.529 --> 00:22:52.529
government. They'll never match each other and an

00:22:52.529 --> 00:22:54.259
unfortunate thing for us is that means

00:22:54.259 --> 00:22:55.259
[inaudible]

00:22:55.259 --> 00:22:59.029
So, what does it look like though? I'm trying to get a sense

00:22:59.049 --> 00:23:00.799
as much as you can tell us...

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:00.980
Sure.

00:23:00.980 --> 00:23:01.980
...for you.

00:23:01.480 --> 00:23:02.480
Oh sure.

00:23:01.980 --> 00:23:03.159
Are you getting penetration?

00:23:03.179 --> 00:23:08.089
Yes. So, for instance we're kind of in a hybrid mode because

00:23:08.109 --> 00:23:11.559
we can exist. One of our requirements is we want to go out

00:23:11.579 --> 00:23:15.289
into the marketplace at least accessible to some code. It's

00:23:15.309 --> 00:23:18.710
much less than what we want, but something. And we design to

00:23:18.730 --> 00:23:22.899
that, so we're okay. And obviously as we're proving value,

00:23:22.919 --> 00:23:25.789
we have Palo Alto Vista is our third biggest customer in the

00:23:25.789 --> 00:23:28.789
country and they're paying the price of the value of the

00:23:28.809 --> 00:23:31.559
test cause it's much less than what they were doing before.

00:23:31.559 --> 00:23:33.059
And they're seeing the value. They're seeing their own

00:23:33.059 --> 00:23:34.059
payer.

00:23:34.059 --> 00:23:36.809
So, when we go to payers who are also integrated systems-

00:23:36.809 --> 00:23:39.769
payer and provider-they get it. They get where we're coming

00:23:39.769 --> 00:23:42.269
at. They're not comparing it. We're dealing with the legacy

00:23:42.269 --> 00:23:44.759
fee for service system and one of the unfortunate things

00:23:44.759 --> 00:23:47.589
even though I'm a physician is that every physician we talk

00:23:47.589 --> 00:23:50.439
to-which is probably the same in Moe's case-"This is a

00:23:50.439 --> 00:23:53.439
better technology. I see the value. I see the data. You have

00:23:53.439 --> 00:23:56.439
10,000 patients. I get it. But I have this equipment. It's

00:23:56.439 --> 00:23:59.689
the worst test, but I'm going to pay for it because the more

00:23:59.689 --> 00:24:01.939
I do of them I got to pay for my small business".

00:24:01.939 --> 00:24:02.939
Yeah.

00:24:02.939 --> 00:24:06.210
That's the reality that everybody agrees is broken. And one

00:24:06.230 --> 00:24:08.939
other thing I think I mentioned that you have to understand,

00:24:08.939 --> 00:24:11.439
a lot of practices are being bought by hospitals. That's

00:24:11.439 --> 00:24:14.799
because I think physicians as a whole are saying, "I went to

00:24:14.819 --> 00:24:18.289
medicine to see patients, not to run a business" and that's

00:24:18.309 --> 00:24:19.779
what increasingly running a business is being a practicing

00:24:19.779 --> 00:24:20.779
physician.

00:24:20.779 --> 00:24:23.919
So, they'd rather be salaried, focus on the patient and let

00:24:23.939 --> 00:24:27.240
someone else handle all the admin, get the economies of

00:24:27.259 --> 00:24:30.129
scale of a big organization. But that's an important thing

00:24:30.149 --> 00:24:32.649
to think about as you're thinking about your ideas because

00:24:32.649 --> 00:24:35.649
even the type of coding going from CPT's, to APC codes, to

00:24:35.649 --> 00:24:39.240
in-patient, to ERG stays depending on how practices merge or

00:24:39.259 --> 00:24:42.430
get acquired are also things you have to think about. So, I

00:24:42.450 --> 00:24:45.869
think everyone approaches reimbursement. There's no way to

00:24:45.889 --> 00:24:49.039
know it until you really get into it and you realize what a

00:24:49.039 --> 00:24:50.039
[inaudible 00:24:50] it is.

00:24:50.039 --> 00:24:54.450
So, hybrid model running, trying to keep the electricity

00:24:54.470 --> 00:24:57.009
down with current sales, but trying to penetrate these

00:24:57.029 --> 00:24:58.029
organizations.

00:24:58.029 --> 00:24:59.679
Oh yeah. Target these high value customers.

00:24:59.679 --> 00:25:00.679
Yeah.

00:25:00.679 --> 00:25:03.179
You see the value, use them to bring the herd.

00:25:03.179 --> 00:25:06.279
Okay, good. Darren? Yeah.

00:25:06.299 --> 00:25:11.269
I put out a different type of hybrid model. So, obviously

00:25:11.289 --> 00:25:16.409
obesity is incredibly a very consumer driven space. I was

00:25:16.429 --> 00:25:19.009
very surprised to hear the statistic that prior to the

00:25:19.029 --> 00:25:23.319
recession 1/3 of patients who were getting a lap band-a

00:25:23.339 --> 00:25:26.879
gastric band for morbid obesity-were actually paying out of

00:25:26.899 --> 00:25:28.169
their own pocket.

00:25:28.189 --> 00:25:31.899
And so, another model that may be a way of doing a hybrid

00:25:31.919 --> 00:25:36.779
approach and getting through the valley of reimbursement is

00:25:36.799 --> 00:25:40.629
turning to consumers when the technology lends itself to

00:25:40.649 --> 00:25:47.169
that as one approach. And frankly, the FDA is probably our

00:25:47.189 --> 00:25:53.649
bigger hurdle even before we get to reimbursement. And

00:25:53.669 --> 00:25:57.099
looking to Europe, looking to other geographies as a good

00:25:57.119 --> 00:26:01.159
launch point I know most obesity companies that are starting

00:26:01.179 --> 00:26:04.069
to bring their companies online are actually going to the U.

00:26:04.089 --> 00:26:08.750
K. And it's like, "Whoa!" Almost no medical devices

00:26:08.769 --> 00:26:13.250
typically start out going up against in the U.K. healthcare

00:26:13.269 --> 00:26:16.359
system where there's a lot of cost effectiveness measures.

00:26:16.379 --> 00:26:21.009
But there's a private pay system there and there's a

00:26:21.029 --> 00:26:22.679
consumer willingness to pay.

00:26:22.699 --> 00:26:27.899
So, there may be an option for technologies that even when

00:26:27.919 --> 00:26:31.299
they have large regulatory hurdles and there's reimbursement

00:26:31.319 --> 00:26:35.909
afterwards to target consumers has a way of getting up the

00:26:35.929 --> 00:26:39.019
curve and getting through that valley of reimbursement.

00:26:39.039 --> 00:26:42.169
So, we'll go back to Europe and global in a little bit. But

00:26:42.169 --> 00:26:44.899
let me ask you specifically, is it part of your strategy?

00:26:44.899 --> 00:26:50.569
Are you in any way practically speaking looking at existing

00:26:50.589 --> 00:26:59.089
ACO's or how fast is this going to happen and what piece of

00:26:59.109 --> 00:27:00.139
your strategy is that?

00:27:00.159 --> 00:27:05.809
So, being a startup of our size it's all about focus. And

00:27:05.829 --> 00:27:09.409
right now that's beyond the regulatory.

00:27:09.429 --> 00:27:10.429
Yeah.

00:27:10.429 --> 00:27:17.109
That's beyond European. And it's in such a state of flux at

00:27:17.129 --> 00:27:21.679
this point that it'll be three or four years till we're

00:27:21.699 --> 00:27:24.509
there. The only thing we're sure about is that the

00:27:24.529 --> 00:27:26.769
landscape's going to look completely different than it does

00:27:26.769 --> 00:27:31.159
now. So, we're going to give that a little time to cook and

00:27:31.179 --> 00:27:33.869
then wait to strategize once it's more solid.

00:27:33.889 --> 00:27:37.730
Okay. Well, that's clear. Moe, I'm going to skip you for a

00:27:37.750 --> 00:27:42.730
sec and ask other Darren. So, what are you seeing as

00:27:42.750 --> 00:27:47.279
successful early stage models with these issues that we're

00:27:47.299 --> 00:27:51.319
talking about? And do you have anybody coming to you? I'm

00:27:51.339 --> 00:27:55.849
kind of fixated on this transition to ACO and whether you're

00:27:55.869 --> 00:28:01.200
seeing anything like that? I think nobody's really doing

00:28:01.220 --> 00:28:04.730
that right now. And I wonder if you see anything?

00:28:04.750 --> 00:28:06.399
We haven't. We haven't seen anything.

00:28:06.399 --> 00:28:07.399
I have.

00:28:07.399 --> 00:28:08.399
He has.

00:28:07.899 --> 00:28:08.899
[overlapping]

00:28:07.899 --> 00:28:12.389
I think it was actually from Moe.

00:28:12.409 --> 00:28:16.429
Right. So, he should probably answer that part. What we are

00:28:16.449 --> 00:28:20.700
seeing though is companies with commercialization plans

00:28:20.720 --> 00:28:24.740
elsewhere first. And we've seen more that aren't in Europe

00:28:24.759 --> 00:28:27.009
first. We've seen more that are in Asia first.

00:28:27.009 --> 00:28:32.389
Yeah. In fact, we just spent two or so weeks in China. And

00:28:32.409 --> 00:28:36.460
on the other Darren's consumer pay point, that's how they're

00:28:36.480 --> 00:28:39.700
paying for their healthcare-especially a lap band type or

00:28:39.720 --> 00:28:43.929
obesity type procedure right now. And there are many, many

00:28:43.949 --> 00:28:47.460
millions of pairs out there. So, we're seeing stuff from

00:28:47.480 --> 00:28:49.409
that angle not really from the ACO angle.

00:28:49.409 --> 00:28:52.659
I'm going to hold you for one more minute and I'm going to

00:28:52.659 --> 00:28:55.960
ask is there anybody in the audience that's with a startup

00:28:55.980 --> 00:29:02.619
or other entity where you have a proactive strategy of

00:29:02.639 --> 00:29:05.789
saying, "Look, healthcare reform is coming. It's going to

00:29:05.809 --> 00:29:10.339
look like these ACO's and we are actively planning for that.

00:29:10.359 --> 00:29:15.789
This is what we're doing" Anybody? Moe?

00:29:15.809 --> 00:29:23.200
What do you want to hear? First of all, what is an ACO? I

00:29:23.220 --> 00:29:25.970
don' think I've heard a clear answer on that. But by

00:29:25.970 --> 00:29:27.970
definition we have ACO's already around greater healthcare

00:29:27.970 --> 00:29:30.470
systems. It's just the thesis around why don't we provide

00:29:30.470 --> 00:29:33.149
quality outcomes? Let's link product solutions interventions

00:29:33.169 --> 00:29:36.889
to cost and quality outcomes vs. the old age adage of

00:29:36.909 --> 00:29:39.669
medical devices which is, "Lets drive volume, let's have

00:29:39.689 --> 00:29:42.949
high margin, let's ring up D.C. for a code and once we're

00:29:42.949 --> 00:29:44.569
okay let's just run the system".

00:29:44.569 --> 00:29:49.369
Would I bet my 30 year career on that thesis? No way. But

00:29:49.389 --> 00:29:52.099
the people I've seen more interested in the ACL model

00:29:52.099 --> 00:29:54.579
unfortunately in the medical device world is more from the

00:29:54.579 --> 00:29:58.210
healthcare IT and services world cause again to enable many

00:29:58.230 --> 00:29:59.339
of these ACO's it's really about data.

00:29:59.339 --> 00:30:00.339
Yeah.

00:30:00.339 --> 00:30:04.500
So, how do we understand decomposition of disease before

00:30:04.519 --> 00:30:07.519
patients feel symptomatic which the wider health world is

00:30:07.539 --> 00:30:10.379
pushing forward. How do we coordinate care? How do we give

00:30:10.399 --> 00:30:13.210
technology to care coordinators to improve those outcomes at

00:30:13.230 --> 00:30:15.019
a fraction of the cost? That's what we're seeing in the

00:30:15.019 --> 00:30:16.019
landscape.

00:30:16.019 --> 00:30:17.899
Well, what's an example or two of that?

00:30:17.899 --> 00:30:22.129
So, again talking to the entrepreneurs in the audience I

00:30:22.149 --> 00:30:25.139
think it's going to be hard to bet everything on an ACO

00:30:25.139 --> 00:30:27.889
without knowing what an ACO is. So, let's start earlier and

00:30:27.889 --> 00:30:31.039
go for some of the binary changes. So, at the end of the

00:30:31.039 --> 00:30:33.789
year 30 day readmission rules for CHF, pneumonia and MI are

00:30:33.789 --> 00:30:36.789
going to change. If that patient comes back within a 30 day

00:30:36.789 --> 00:30:39.539
period the hospital will not be paid again. In the classic

00:30:39.539 --> 00:30:42.539
fee for service system they did, so it was actually good for

00:30:42.539 --> 00:30:44.289
that patient to keep on coming back.

00:30:44.289 --> 00:30:46.539
Again, it shows how asinine the reimbursement system is.

00:30:46.539 --> 00:30:49.740
Their senses are so misaligned. So, we've seen a lot of

00:30:49.759 --> 00:30:53.579
activity around that rule change and hospitals are getting

00:30:53.599 --> 00:30:55.599
very interested in solutions that solve that particular

00:30:55.599 --> 00:30:57.959
problem. As time goes on you have bundle payments for other

00:30:57.959 --> 00:31:02.609
conditions. The ACO, I can predict what I think will happen

00:31:02.629 --> 00:31:05.629
when it does kick off, but I believe the biggest change will

00:31:05.639 --> 00:31:08.809
be the distribution channel that these guys are going to

00:31:08.829 --> 00:31:09.829
have to go through.

00:31:09.829 --> 00:31:12.829
Again, the thesis of, "I'm going to have to sell to a

00:31:12.829 --> 00:31:15.579
physician and show him or her good data, and they're going

00:31:15.579 --> 00:31:18.829
to make a lot of money off that device. I think those days

00:31:18.829 --> 00:31:20.079
are also going to go.

00:31:20.079 --> 00:31:22.079
[inaudible] physicians are hiring them as they said. The

00:31:22.079 --> 00:31:24.579
ACO's are going to be more centralized in purchasing, so

00:31:24.579 --> 00:31:27.000
essentially what Kaiser are doing already. So, I think the

00:31:27.000 --> 00:31:30.089
biggest flux out of this will be the channel to sell stuff

00:31:30.109 --> 00:31:32.309
and the mechanism one has to do that.

00:31:32.329 --> 00:31:33.329
Maybe I can...

00:31:33.329 --> 00:31:34.349
Yeah go ahead.

00:31:34.369 --> 00:31:38.269
...talk about the ACL model and pick up on what you said

00:31:38.289 --> 00:31:39.289
about data.

00:31:39.289 --> 00:31:40.289
Yeah.

00:31:40.289 --> 00:31:42.539
I used Ireland as an example. So, if any of you feel

00:31:42.539 --> 00:31:45.039
lightheaded or dizzy and you go to Stanford emergency room

00:31:45.039 --> 00:31:48.289
they'll put on our patch. They'll put it on you if they can'

00:31:48.289 --> 00:31:51.289
t figure out what's going on. You'll go home and that's an

00:31:51.289 --> 00:31:53.539
emergency room physician taking care of a potential disease

00:31:53.539 --> 00:31:56.289
state possibly due to an abnormal heart rhythm. If you go

00:31:56.289 --> 00:31:59.039
home, go about your business, maybe you had whatever you had

00:31:59.039 --> 00:32:02.039
on day seven, or 10, or whatever. It gets recorded, you mail

00:32:02.039 --> 00:32:04.789
this back to us, we process the report. That report goes

00:32:04.789 --> 00:32:06.789
back to Stanford's cardiologists who are reading these

00:32:06.789 --> 00:32:09.789
reports on a day to day rotation. They'll look at it. They'

00:32:09.789 --> 00:32:13.039
ll see that you had your system, they'll see that you had a

00:32:13.039 --> 00:32:15.539
normal heart rhythm. They'll call you and say, "Okay". Now

00:32:15.539 --> 00:32:17.539
it's a different physician managing information that started

00:32:17.539 --> 00:32:19.289
with a different physician at the beginning.

00:32:19.289 --> 00:32:22.289
They'll look at that and say, "Okay. You need to come visit

00:32:22.289 --> 00:32:25.039
us." They'll see you and say, "This is treatable. Come see

00:32:25.039 --> 00:32:27.289
an electro physiologist .like me and you'll get treated."

00:32:27.289 --> 00:32:30.539
Once you see me and you get treated I might put another one

00:32:30.539 --> 00:32:33.789
of these on to see if what I did actually had the desired

00:32:33.789 --> 00:32:37.039
effect. That's an ACO with what we do in a sense that we're

00:32:37.039 --> 00:32:39.990
managing a disease state enabling by a device that's really

00:32:40.009 --> 00:32:42.970
centralized about information movement and management.

00:32:42.990 --> 00:32:45.869
I mean, I almost think of our company as really an IT

00:32:45.869 --> 00:32:48.369
company enabled by a MedTech device. Every one of these

00:32:48.369 --> 00:32:51.119
things over 14 days collects a million heartbeats and I know

00:32:51.119 --> 00:32:53.119
everything that happened with those million heartbeats. I

00:32:53.119 --> 00:32:55.619
know which physician prescribed it, I know what they find.

00:32:55.619 --> 00:32:58.119
So, there's a lot of business intelligence, but again I

00:32:58.119 --> 00:33:00.369
think it's the idea of leveraging information which is

00:33:00.369 --> 00:33:03.490
really not expensive vs. leveraging things and moving

00:33:03.509 --> 00:33:06.490
patients which is expensive, is where things are going.

00:33:06.509 --> 00:33:10.409
So, on a high level that's kind of a high pitch and everyone

00:33:10.429 --> 00:33:13.589
gets it because that's much more rationale. Now, we'll just

00:33:13.609 --> 00:33:14.859
see how that gets implemented.

00:33:14.109 --> 00:33:15.109
Yeah.

00:33:15.109 --> 00:33:16.359
Can I add one more thing to that?

00:33:16.359 --> 00:33:17.359
Yeah.

00:33:17.359 --> 00:33:19.359
[inaudible] very well. So, the classic device experience I

00:33:19.359 --> 00:33:22.699
had was, "Here's the product and we will sell it". That's

00:33:22.719 --> 00:33:26.189
changing as Udai said more of a technology or a service with

00:33:26.209 --> 00:33:29.369
the device. It's again linked to specific outcomes that you'

00:33:29.389 --> 00:33:32.720
re trying to achieve. And that's the big shift that I see.

00:33:32.740 --> 00:33:36.589
So, Udai's company is a good example of this. Can you

00:33:36.609 --> 00:33:41.169
brainstorm? And Moe, you're seeing this. I mean, there's got

00:33:41.189 --> 00:33:47.429
to be a wider zone than just wireless parameters of

00:33:47.449 --> 00:33:48.819
heartbeat digestion, breathing...

00:33:48.839 --> 00:33:49.839
Yeah.

00:33:49.839 --> 00:33:52.649
So, we can see those. But where else are we going?

00:33:52.669 --> 00:33:55.909
So, the way that we're dividing the universe-so devices are

00:33:55.929 --> 00:33:58.679
just the mechanism to capture data. But that by itself is

00:33:58.679 --> 00:34:01.869
nothing apart from if you can really capture novel data. So,

00:34:01.889 --> 00:34:05.309
if you can pick decomposition of CHF two years in advance

00:34:05.329 --> 00:34:09.929
that no one can do, great. But a necessity in actually most

00:34:09.949 --> 00:34:12.440
devices are more simple. So, that means you have to think

00:34:12.440 --> 00:34:14.940
about how that data is transmitted. So, that's the wireless

00:34:14.940 --> 00:34:17.690
world. How is data stored? What are the analytics that you

00:34:17.690 --> 00:34:20.190
apply to data to get the information? That's the healthcare

00:34:20.190 --> 00:34:22.940
IT world. And then how do you present that information at

00:34:22.940 --> 00:34:25.940
the right time to the right person in a new user interface?

00:34:25.940 --> 00:34:28.309
And that's a convergence of 3-4 different industries which

00:34:28.309 --> 00:34:29.759
no one's really figured out yet.

00:34:29.759 --> 00:34:32.710
So, there's some early players who are essentially making

00:34:32.730 --> 00:34:35.829
the competence around data. I firmly believe there's a

00:34:35.849 --> 00:34:38.599
Google to be built within healthcare who can really

00:34:38.619 --> 00:34:43.190
understand this data coming from multisource whether it's

00:34:43.210 --> 00:34:45.210
genomics, devices, clinical data and then really turn it

00:34:45.210 --> 00:34:46.210
into something meaningful.

00:34:46.210 --> 00:34:49.210
And if you push me to where I think the biggest opportunity

00:34:49.210 --> 00:34:51.960
is post healthcare reform- and I was talking to Darren about

00:34:51.960 --> 00:34:54.460
this-providers are now at risk. And I've heard through the

00:34:54.460 --> 00:34:57.210
grapevine some pairs actually want to get out of the classic

00:34:57.210 --> 00:34:59.710
insurance business. They don't want to be reinsurers to the

00:34:59.710 --> 00:35:02.960
ACL model who will be at risk. And I think that's a really

00:35:02.960 --> 00:35:05.460
interesting concept. And you can see that on the landscape.

00:35:05.460 --> 00:35:08.089
My caveat is this is all timing. How long will it take for

00:35:08.089 --> 00:35:10.829
this to all settle down? And I think it's more years rather

00:35:10.829 --> 00:35:14.809
than months which makes it very difficult as an entrepreneur

00:35:14.829 --> 00:35:17.579
in terms of your business model. What do you want to build

00:35:17.579 --> 00:35:18.579
your company for?

00:35:18.579 --> 00:35:21.239
But I'll go back to the basic fundamentals and the

00:35:21.239 --> 00:35:23.299
statistics around healthcare spending are terrible as you

00:35:23.319 --> 00:35:27.119
know. 2065 we'll spend 100% of GDP on healthcare. That's not

00:35:27.139 --> 00:35:30.130
going to happen. Things will fail way before then. So, if we

00:35:30.130 --> 00:35:32.889
really focus on product solutions that can help reduce the

00:35:32.909 --> 00:35:35.929
cost which will get enumerated in the business models

00:35:35.949 --> 00:35:38.590
emerging I think that's a good bet to place.

00:35:38.610 --> 00:35:45.650
So, I smell a contrarian situation here which is every time

00:35:45.670 --> 00:35:51.069
I hear value based innovation talked about it has to do with

00:35:51.089 --> 00:35:54.400
the interface with information technology and it's just the

00:35:54.420 --> 00:35:55.420
classic example.

00:35:55.420 --> 00:35:56.420
Sure.

00:35:56.420 --> 00:36:00.069
You're the classic example. And then you see something like

00:36:00.089 --> 00:36:01.089
[inaudible], right?

00:36:01.089 --> 00:36:02.089
Sure.

00:36:01.369 --> 00:36:04.759
So, is anybody familiar enough with

00:36:04.779 --> 00:36:08.409
[inaudible] to tell the story? Darren do you know that one

00:36:08.429 --> 00:36:16.150
well enough? I won't pick on you. Okay. So, basically this

00:36:16.170 --> 00:36:20.569
is a local company that just sold very attractively to

00:36:20.589 --> 00:36:24.869
Medtronic that came up with a catheter based solution for

00:36:24.889 --> 00:36:29.449
hypertension. And it's kind of based on some classic

00:36:29.469 --> 00:36:34.440
physiologic studies that were done years ago where surgeons

00:36:34.460 --> 00:36:39.989
would actually cut the nerves that go to the kidney. The

00:36:40.009 --> 00:36:42.509
kidney as you know is involved in regulating blood pressure.

00:36:42.509 --> 00:36:48.269
So, as a last ditch procedure if you cut those nerves you

00:36:48.289 --> 00:36:50.210
could drop the blood pressure.

00:36:50.230 --> 00:36:54.980
Company The Foundry, picked up on that, developed a catheter

00:36:55.000 --> 00:37:01.589
based strategy for doing this. And the cost implications of

00:37:01.609 --> 00:37:04.440
that are absolutely phenomenal, right? So, they have three

00:37:04.460 --> 00:37:12.319
year good data on reducing blood pressure. And so, the

00:37:12.339 --> 00:37:16.690
contrarian play that I was talking about is that I

00:37:16.710 --> 00:37:20.389
absolutely feel it in my water that there are great

00:37:20.409 --> 00:37:22.750
technologies-simple technologies-that have not been

00:37:22.769 --> 00:37:26.269
invented, that are cost saving. And Darren, you may have one

00:37:26.289 --> 00:37:27.960
of those, right?

00:37:27.980 --> 00:37:28.980
Absolutely.

00:37:28.230 --> 00:37:29.230
So...

00:37:29.230 --> 00:37:31.559
But I think you can think about it different ways. I mean,

00:37:31.559 --> 00:37:35.059
we talked about it and I think one of the things I think at

00:37:35.059 --> 00:37:37.750
the government level-and this goes back to my comment about

00:37:37.750 --> 00:37:42.269
payers- what I gave and what Darren, and what Moe and I were

00:37:42.289 --> 00:37:44.289
talking about made me happy with diagnostics and

00:37:44.289 --> 00:37:45.699
particularly information management.

00:37:45.719 --> 00:37:48.769
So, again if you're thinking of an ACL model in terms of

00:37:48.769 --> 00:37:51.679
outcomes I break things down to four big buckets. You have

00:37:51.699 --> 00:37:55.230
an acute diagnostic that is changing. You can change like

00:37:55.250 --> 00:37:59.460
what I do-changing when something is diagnosed. If it

00:37:59.480 --> 00:38:02.199
obviates multiple tests, multiple visits, repeat tests to

00:38:02.219 --> 00:38:04.289
get to the same outcome, that's cost saving.

00:38:04.289 --> 00:38:08.130
You can have acute therapies. So, it's like an

00:38:08.150 --> 00:38:10.710
[inaudible] You have a onetime therapy, but to really value

00:38:10.730 --> 00:38:13.369
it's outcome you have to look across the disease state of

00:38:13.369 --> 00:38:16.230
the patient which means not only treating them now what'd it

00:38:16.230 --> 00:38:18.710
prevent, but also what'd it increase in terms of their

00:38:18.710 --> 00:38:21.679
productivity-in terms of their ability to work, not be on

00:38:21.699 --> 00:38:22.699
time, etc.

00:38:22.699 --> 00:38:25.460
Then on the chronic you have things like chronic

00:38:25.480 --> 00:38:28.809
therapeutics-taking a pill every day, getting dialysis three

00:38:28.829 --> 00:38:31.900
times a week. You have to look at what are the outcome

00:38:31.920 --> 00:38:34.920
effects on the disease state to get to the outcome model and

00:38:34.920 --> 00:38:37.719
then there's-well, we talked about chronic therapy. Those

00:38:37.739 --> 00:38:43.639
are kind of the buckets that you can think about-acute point

00:38:43.659 --> 00:38:46.630
in time, chronic point in time. It's like putting in a

00:38:46.630 --> 00:38:49.079
defibrillator-one time shot. People say it's very expensive.

00:38:49.079 --> 00:38:53.400
But we know we're agreed as a U.S.-and Stephanie will tell

00:38:53.420 --> 00:38:56.670
us this-that we agree to $50,000 a year of dialysis to get

00:38:56.670 --> 00:38:58.670
quality of life care going forward. Defibrillators-20,000-

00:38:58.670 --> 00:39:01.670
30,000 saves lives, but how is that measured?

00:39:01.690 --> 00:39:02.690
Yeah.

00:39:02.690 --> 00:39:04.599
On an annual budget cycle it's not a worthwhile thing.

00:39:04.599 --> 00:39:05.599
Yeah.

00:39:05.599 --> 00:39:08.519
But over the course of several years it is. And I think that

00:39:08.519 --> 00:39:11.049
guidelines support that ACC doesn't recommend putting it

00:39:11.069 --> 00:39:13.719
where someone's happier to live. So, I do think there are

00:39:13.719 --> 00:39:16.650
outcomes that are not information only that tie to outcomes

00:39:16.670 --> 00:39:19.789
based ACO's. It's just that we have to define them better

00:39:19.789 --> 00:39:21.089
particular to technology.

00:39:21.109 --> 00:39:25.500
Yeah. Good. We had a question in the front and I got blinded

00:39:25.519 --> 00:39:27.039
by the light. I'm sorry about that.

00:39:27.039 --> 00:39:29.099
That's okay. I'm

00:39:29.119 --> 00:39:32.769
[inaudible] I'm a physician from India and a student at

00:39:32.789 --> 00:39:35.529
Stanford. So, this is for the physicians out there. Just in

00:39:35.529 --> 00:39:39.679
terms of the opportunity cost, how valuable would you say

00:39:39.699 --> 00:39:42.900
has the advanced medical training been vs. just the four

00:39:42.920 --> 00:39:49.069
years of med school being in the industry in terms of giving

00:39:49.089 --> 00:39:50.089
you...

00:39:50.089 --> 00:39:51.339
Do you want to start?

00:39:51.339 --> 00:39:52.339
Yeah, I'll start.

00:39:52.339 --> 00:39:53.339
...a leverage there?

00:39:53.339 --> 00:39:55.839
Some people thought it was useless. I think it really

00:39:55.859 --> 00:39:58.599
depends on fit. So, if someone's looking for a clinical

00:39:58.599 --> 00:40:01.250
person they'll find you. But the hardest issue I find in

00:40:01.250 --> 00:40:03.750
transition is being a clinical person and trying to enter

00:40:03.750 --> 00:40:08.130
into the business world again because of supply/demand

00:40:08.150 --> 00:40:10.969
preconceptions. And again, that was in the classic MedTech

00:40:10.989 --> 00:40:13.739
world and then as soon as I just sort of serendipitously

00:40:13.739 --> 00:40:17.089
stepped sort of sideways to an industry which was growing

00:40:17.109 --> 00:40:20.819
and evolving that didn't have a huge supply of physicians I

00:40:20.839 --> 00:40:22.839
got a lot more traction.

00:40:22.859 --> 00:40:27.099
I mean, I think for me I took a year off after med school to

00:40:27.119 --> 00:40:29.869
see if I really liked device development. I realized at the

00:40:29.869 --> 00:40:33.779
end of the day going to med school you learn something.

00:40:33.799 --> 00:40:36.799
Taking care of people, and coding them at 5:00 in the

00:40:36.819 --> 00:40:40.069
morning when no one else is around in an 18 bed CCU? That's

00:40:40.069 --> 00:40:42.569
being a physician. So, they're two different things and I

00:40:42.569 --> 00:40:45.069
think if you really want to understand unmet medical needs

00:40:45.069 --> 00:40:47.819
in your field and you want to focus more on understanding

00:40:47.819 --> 00:40:50.569
the needs, figuring out how to solve those problems it is

00:40:50.569 --> 00:40:52.900
helpful. The flip side of that is it's a long road.

00:40:52.900 --> 00:40:56.579
And again, being able to practice and do all these things is

00:40:56.599 --> 00:40:59.599
a balancing act. But I think it really depends on as Moe

00:40:59.599 --> 00:41:02.349
said, the right fit. I mean, if you're really going to

00:41:02.349 --> 00:41:04.849
transition more to the business side maybe it's not as

00:41:04.849 --> 00:41:07.599
important. But if you really want to be more from the

00:41:07.599 --> 00:41:10.599
clinical insight and again, what we talk a lot about is the

00:41:10.599 --> 00:41:13.599
design experience. I mean, we've tried to do a lot with the

00:41:13.599 --> 00:41:16.099
user experience for the patient, the physician. But I know

00:41:16.099 --> 00:41:18.849
that because I've been there, not on the physician side. But

00:41:18.849 --> 00:41:20.849
it's really, really important to understand and empathize

00:41:20.849 --> 00:41:23.599
with those things because that's where you see some of those

00:41:23.599 --> 00:41:25.849
insights and unless you've been there quite a few times it's

00:41:25.849 --> 00:41:26.849
sometimes hard.

00:41:26.849 --> 00:41:29.599
So, I don't think there's a right answer. I really thing, "

00:41:29.599 --> 00:41:32.360
Are you going to go more business oriented, or perhaps stay

00:41:32.380 --> 00:41:34.880
a little bit more medically focused and keep doing the

00:41:34.880 --> 00:41:35.929
clinical invention side.

00:41:35.949 --> 00:41:37.779
I'll give that one a shot too...

00:41:37.779 --> 00:41:38.779
Yeah?

00:41:38.779 --> 00:41:41.029
...cause I was in medical school and left. So...

00:41:41.029 --> 00:41:43.829
He's the smartest one of us all.

00:41:43.849 --> 00:41:45.980
Yeah.

00:41:46.000 --> 00:41:47.730
He's a venture capitalist by the way. So...

00:41:47.730 --> 00:41:53.759
So, another statistic. Yeah, go ahead.

00:41:53.779 --> 00:41:57.369
So, I think it just really comes down to if you like being

00:41:57.389 --> 00:42:00.869
in medical school stay in medical school. I mean, honest to

00:42:00.889 --> 00:42:05.000
God. And with the years you're spending in medical school

00:42:05.019 --> 00:42:07.619
and you're probably paying a lot of money. You're working

00:42:07.619 --> 00:42:10.460
really, really hard and you're in the library by yourself

00:42:10.480 --> 00:42:16.440
with a book at 2:00 in the morning, If that's driving you

00:42:16.460 --> 00:42:19.059
and you want to take care of patients, stay in medical

00:42:19.059 --> 00:42:26.699
school. If you don't we should talk more afterwards. But if

00:42:26.719 --> 00:42:28.480
you don't, do you need it?

00:42:28.500 --> 00:42:29.500
No.

00:42:29.500 --> 00:42:33.250
I don't think you need an M.D. I don't think you need it. It

00:42:33.250 --> 00:42:35.750
helps. You get called a doctor. You get respect. You've

00:42:35.750 --> 00:42:38.250
earned the respect. You don't need it though. But it

00:42:38.250 --> 00:42:41.000
depends. You've got to like what you're doing at that time.

00:42:41.000 --> 00:42:43.750
So, from the perspective of you want to have the patients

00:42:43.750 --> 00:42:48.750
running new devices, I'm going to try to get it more towards

00:42:48.769 --> 00:42:51.779
probably Moe and Dr.

00:42:51.799 --> 00:42:52.799
[inaudible] Would you do it again?

00:42:52.799 --> 00:42:57.089
Oh yeah, definitely. I mean, I think it's I would say less

00:42:57.109 --> 00:42:59.839
about inventing devices, but really understanding where the

00:42:59.859 --> 00:43:02.609
needs of healthcare are. I mean, unless you've like I said

00:43:02.609 --> 00:43:04.859
taken care of patients, understood all the craziness that

00:43:04.859 --> 00:43:08.259
happens in a hospital, or in a clinic, or seeing patients

00:43:08.279 --> 00:43:11.279
come from far away with their loved ones, or having to give

00:43:11.279 --> 00:43:14.250
someone bad news, that's where you see needs. You don't have

00:43:14.250 --> 00:43:17.250
to be a physician, but you can see needs from the physician

00:43:17.250 --> 00:43:19.259
perspective. You can still see needs in a very important

00:43:19.259 --> 00:43:22.489
way. If you're not a physician no question, from a business

00:43:22.509 --> 00:43:23.849
perspective and engineering perspective.

00:43:23.869 --> 00:43:27.049
But again, in trying to understand, "What do you like doing?

00:43:27.069 --> 00:43:30.319
" At the end of the day, I mean, when you're in your late

00:43:30.319 --> 00:43:32.819
20's, early 30's that should have narrowed down somewhat to

00:43:32.819 --> 00:43:35.569
what fits with your personality and what you like doing. The

00:43:35.569 --> 00:43:38.569
world is not always like the Art of Choosing that we heard

00:43:38.569 --> 00:43:40.449
this morning. You have to make a choice.

00:43:40.449 --> 00:43:41.449
Thank you.

00:43:41.449 --> 00:43:44.359
So, I heard a statistic earlier this week that there are

00:43:44.379 --> 00:43:48.579
about 100 medical students at Stanford and 27 want business

00:43:48.599 --> 00:43:52.059
degrees. So, I think that may be a referendum on healthcare

00:43:52.079 --> 00:43:55.589
reform. I'm not sure. But Darren, you were going to say

00:43:55.609 --> 00:43:57.049
something else?

00:43:57.069 --> 00:43:58.069
[Overlapping]

00:43:58.069 --> 00:44:02.739
I think the question of what do we really want to do with

00:44:02.759 --> 00:44:08.630
our lives is one for all of us. And there's a lot of talk

00:44:08.650 --> 00:44:11.949
amongst colleagues here about, "Why are you spending your

00:44:11.969 --> 00:44:16.460
life doing a Class 3 PMA device? A product? I mean, this is

00:44:16.480 --> 00:44:19.130
something that doesn't fly around in the air."

00:44:19.150 --> 00:44:23.929
And when it comes down to it this is a medical need...

00:44:23.949 --> 00:44:24.949
Yeah.

00:44:24.949 --> 00:44:28.529
...that I thought was incredibly worthwhile. I had veterans

00:44:28.549 --> 00:44:32.849
in the industry that had worked at obesity startups telling

00:44:32.869 --> 00:44:36.440
me, "Don't do it". They were like, "You may waste five

00:44:36.460 --> 00:44:39.819
years, you may waste a decade of your life" because that's

00:44:39.839 --> 00:44:43.920
just a feeling that they had working at their companies.

00:44:43.940 --> 00:44:47.630
And at the end of the day you look out there. You're like, '

00:44:47.650 --> 00:44:50.150
There are 20 million Americans-not even looking at the rest

00:44:50.150 --> 00:44:53.539
of the world, just Americans-that are eligible for bariatric

00:44:53.559 --> 00:44:57.079
surgery for these weight loss procedures. And less than 2%

00:44:57.099 --> 00:45:00.969
of them actually get it." And you talk to the patients who

00:45:00.989 --> 00:45:04.599
are making these gut wrenching decisions about you. "Do they

00:45:04.619 --> 00:45:07.619
really want to go under the knife? Do they not? What's this

00:45:07.619 --> 00:45:10.789
going to mean for their families?" This is really an area

00:45:10.809 --> 00:45:13.359
that needs to be changed.

00:45:13.379 --> 00:45:17.869
And it's worthwhile. Thankfully I was able to convince some

00:45:17.889 --> 00:45:23.109
investors it was also worthwhile. And it's going to be

00:45:23.129 --> 00:45:25.719
leading up to helping patients now.

00:45:25.739 --> 00:45:26.739
Yeah.

00:45:26.739 --> 00:45:29.739
Can I just add something? So, if you don't feel that passion

00:45:29.739 --> 00:45:31.989
about what you're talking about with healthcare I wouldn't

00:45:31.989 --> 00:45:34.739
do it. It's pretty timely now. Our friends from Facebook are

00:45:34.739 --> 00:45:37.699
all retiring and buying lofts in New York. And healthcare is

00:45:37.719 --> 00:45:40.500
so fragmented and regulated that you're not going to get

00:45:40.519 --> 00:45:42.629
those same type of opportunities. Compared to tech, you

00:45:42.629 --> 00:45:45.069
really have to be passionate about this space which I think

00:45:45.069 --> 00:45:46.069
we all are.

00:45:46.069 --> 00:45:49.319
I think one of the things I would ask Darren, I think none

00:45:49.319 --> 00:45:52.819
of us would disagree with is that at the end of the day if

00:45:52.819 --> 00:45:55.319
none of our companies had succeeded, I wouldn't have traded

00:45:55.319 --> 00:45:57.819
the experience for anything in terms of what I've learned.

00:45:57.819 --> 00:46:00.569
And I think that's really important. You really have to go

00:46:00.569 --> 00:46:02.819
into it thinking about, "What am I going to learn from this

00:46:02.819 --> 00:46:03.819
experience?"

00:46:03.819 --> 00:46:06.319
And give yourself some reasonable time limits. I mean, don't

00:46:06.319 --> 00:46:09.319
say, "I'm going to do this for 20 years." But really focus

00:46:09.319 --> 00:46:12.069
and say, "When I started the company I gave myself 6-12

00:46:12.069 --> 00:46:14.819
months to get funded. If I couldn't get funded maybe it

00:46:14.819 --> 00:46:17.569
wasn't the right time." You really have to set limits. But

00:46:17.569 --> 00:46:20.569
if you do, and you keep going, and going, and you're seeing

00:46:20.569 --> 00:46:23.319
progress, at the end of the day that's invaluable. And from

00:46:23.319 --> 00:46:25.569
a company standpoint experience means more than anything in

00:46:25.569 --> 00:46:26.819
terms of who we hire.

00:46:26.069 --> 00:46:27.069
Yes?

00:46:27.069 --> 00:46:30.069
Hi. My name's Edith. I'm a Ph.D. student in Engineering here

00:46:30.069 --> 00:46:32.569
and I also have another career related question. At Stanford

00:46:32.569 --> 00:46:34.819
it's so entrepreneurial. It seems like there's this baseline

00:46:34.819 --> 00:46:37.930
assumption of, "Of course everyone wants to start a company"

00:46:37.950 --> 00:46:38.150
.

00:46:38.150 --> 00:46:39.299
And we call it Google-itess.

00:46:39.299 --> 00:46:44.949
Yeah, exactly. But starting a company vs. maybe tackling

00:46:44.969 --> 00:46:47.159
some of those problems that you're passionate about as

00:46:47.159 --> 00:46:50.619
employee #10 or as part of a larger organization, very

00:46:50.639 --> 00:46:53.420
different situations and a lot of tradeoffs, and I was

00:46:53.440 --> 00:46:55.909
wondering if you could talk about the decision process to

00:46:55.909 --> 00:46:59.539
decide, "To do what I want to do I have to start a company"

00:46:59.559 --> 00:47:02.449
and how you weighed some of those tradeoffs for your

00:47:02.469 --> 00:47:03.469
personal situation.

00:47:03.469 --> 00:47:04.469
Somebody want to lead?

00:47:04.469 --> 00:47:11.059
Yeah. I started my career at Medtronic, not a small company

00:47:11.079 --> 00:47:15.809
by any stretch of the imagination. And I found that it was a

00:47:15.829 --> 00:47:21.029
fantastic environment. And even though I was in a 30,000

00:47:21.049 --> 00:47:24.880
person organization I found myself gravitating towards

00:47:24.900 --> 00:47:27.799
looking at challenging problems that I thought just needed

00:47:27.819 --> 00:47:32.150
to be solved. So, I ended up doing entrepreneurship inside

00:47:32.170 --> 00:47:34.670
of Medtronic which is a wonderful place to start because

00:47:34.670 --> 00:47:38.559
here you have all this infrastructure, all this money and

00:47:38.579 --> 00:47:42.429
all these incredible people that can help you bring

00:47:42.449 --> 00:47:45.480
something to fruition.

00:47:45.500 --> 00:47:50.079
But there's pros and cons to that. At the time I was 22

00:47:50.099 --> 00:47:53.559
years old trying to work inside of a very large bureaucracy

00:47:53.579 --> 00:47:56.960
and getting buy-in that the President of the organization

00:47:56.980 --> 00:48:00.909
that, "Yeah. You should give me $6 million and 50 people to

00:48:00.929 --> 00:48:05.190
help bring this new product to market". And that process

00:48:05.210 --> 00:48:10.539
took 6-9 months. It wasn't like actually fundraising.

00:48:10.559 --> 00:48:15.309
That's a big chunk of change.

00:48:15.329 --> 00:48:21.670
Yeah. And so, that brought the product to market. It was a

00:48:21.690 --> 00:48:25.190
fantastic opportunity. But what I learned from that

00:48:25.210 --> 00:48:30.349
entrepreneurial experience is what would it be like to do

00:48:30.369 --> 00:48:35.119
the entrepreneurship experience?

00:48:35.139 --> 00:48:39.509
I remember we spent $250,000 on five lines of code because

00:48:39.529 --> 00:48:42.279
there were some programmers in Lafayette that were the only

00:48:42.299 --> 00:48:45.489
people that could do this in the world. Then I'd look at

00:48:45.489 --> 00:48:50.190
what I did with the first $250,000 at Hourglass and it's

00:48:50.210 --> 00:48:54.099
night and day. We kind of starting proving in some pre-

00:48:54.119 --> 00:48:57.509
clinical models that this could be a brand new therapy for

00:48:57.529 --> 00:48:58.949
patients.

00:48:58.969 --> 00:49:04.779
So, large companies definitely provide a great, great

00:49:04.799 --> 00:49:09.009
learning experience. But there's nothing quite like starting

00:49:09.029 --> 00:49:13.029
something from scratch and something that the world's never

00:49:13.049 --> 00:49:14.049
seen before.

00:49:14.049 --> 00:49:18.009
We shouldn't also underestimate that all of this is a huge

00:49:18.029 --> 00:49:22.730
element of luck. It's a lot of being in the right place at

00:49:22.750 --> 00:49:25.500
the right time. So, I think it's more about creating the

00:49:25.500 --> 00:49:28.500
opportunities for you to be in the right place at the right

00:49:28.500 --> 00:49:31.250
time. It goes back to what I was saying earlier. Everyone

00:49:31.250 --> 00:49:34.000
can be an entrepreneur and there's a lot of really good

00:49:34.000 --> 00:49:36.500
ideas. But there's not as many successful companies. And a

00:49:36.500 --> 00:49:39.000
lot of that has to do with things which are outside of your

00:49:39.000 --> 00:49:40.000
control.

00:49:40.000 --> 00:49:42.500
So, I think it's more about getting the right experience

00:49:42.500 --> 00:49:44.750
that makes you passionate about whatever you're doing and

00:49:44.750 --> 00:49:46.750
hopefully that'll lead to providing the opportunities and

00:49:46.750 --> 00:49:49.549
experiences that will then afford a higher likelihood of

00:49:49.569 --> 00:49:51.819
getting that entrepreneurial experience if that's what you

00:49:50.539 --> 00:49:51.539
seek.

00:49:51.539 --> 00:49:55.119
So, let's shift gears. Did you have a comment?

00:49:55.139 --> 00:50:00.219
No, just one quick thing on that is you're always better off

00:50:00.239 --> 00:50:02.599
when you surround yourself with good people-people who care

00:50:02.599 --> 00:50:05.089
about you, people who have done it. Whether you're starting

00:50:05.089 --> 00:50:07.589
your own company and they're advising you or whether you're

00:50:07.589 --> 00:50:10.589
in a big company and working for somebody as long as they

00:50:10.589 --> 00:50:13.589
know you're there, care about you, and want to help you you'

00:50:13.589 --> 00:50:15.839
re always going to be better off I think.

00:50:15.839 --> 00:50:18.589
So, I want to move to talking about global strategies. I'm

00:50:18.589 --> 00:50:21.589
going to ask you each to think about what you've done in

00:50:21.589 --> 00:50:25.549
your own businesses and I want to invite people in the

00:50:25.569 --> 00:50:30.339
audience too. We have some experienced folks and if you want

00:50:30.359 --> 00:50:34.289
to contribute to this discussion please do. But let me

00:50:34.309 --> 00:50:37.719
start. Moe, I actually don't know what you would say about

00:50:37.739 --> 00:50:41.119
global strategies. So, let's start with you.

00:50:41.139 --> 00:50:45.589
We actually have a product that we're about to spin out. I'

00:50:45.609 --> 00:50:48.859
ll give you our thesis first. So, you may have heard some of

00:50:48.859 --> 00:50:51.359
the other speakers this morning, but there are actually more

00:50:51.359 --> 00:50:53.859
mobile phones in the world than shoes. And people have

00:50:53.859 --> 00:50:56.109
access to phones easier than clean water. Countries in

00:50:56.109 --> 00:50:58.359
Africa and India are building their healthcare systems from

00:50:58.359 --> 00:51:01.000
the ground up without ever going to have the number of

00:51:01.000 --> 00:51:03.500
physicians that we have here or these technologies. So, it's

00:51:03.500 --> 00:51:06.359
an interesting place to see what's going on and something we

00:51:06.359 --> 00:51:07.359
want to capitalize on.

00:51:07.359 --> 00:51:11.449
So, I think the thesis of high volume/low margin devices and

00:51:11.469 --> 00:51:14.259
solutions vs. what we see here in the U.S. is a real

00:51:14.259 --> 00:51:18.079
opportunity. So, we have a low cost maternal health solution

00:51:18.099 --> 00:51:21.969
that we're still prototyping and at a certain point that

00:51:21.989 --> 00:51:24.989
will be spun out. It's going live in Mexico first and then

00:51:24.989 --> 00:51:27.440
India and Brazil. And essentially it's not rocket science.

00:51:27.440 --> 00:51:29.210
It's very simple, frugal innovation.

00:51:29.230 --> 00:51:31.730
The gold standard right now in a hospital is a

00:51:31.730 --> 00:51:34.400
cardiotocogram between $5,000-10,000. We have a couple

00:51:34.420 --> 00:51:37.879
hundred dollars version which is blue tooth enabled. You

00:51:37.899 --> 00:51:40.899
don't need a physician at the point of care. A nurse can

00:51:40.899 --> 00:51:43.649
carry that around. The data goes to a centralized place and

00:51:43.649 --> 00:51:46.750
we're bundling that with blood glucose, blood pressure and

00:51:46.769 --> 00:51:49.539
urine dipsticks. So again, not rocket science stuff, but

00:51:49.559 --> 00:51:52.199
will have a huge impact in maternal health and mortality we

00:51:52.199 --> 00:51:53.449
hope.

00:51:53.469 --> 00:51:58.139
Darren hi, so companies coming to you. What percentage have

00:51:58.159 --> 00:52:03.809
a Europe only strategy and how are you assessing that?

00:52:03.829 --> 00:52:07.559
None have a Europe only strategy. We did a thing recently

00:52:07.579 --> 00:52:10.230
where we looked at the companies we currently invested in

00:52:10.250 --> 00:52:14.170
and every one of them on the device side was somewhere else

00:52:14.190 --> 00:52:16.629
in the world other than the United States either selling, or

00:52:16.629 --> 00:52:20.859
manufacturing, or testing, or every single one was

00:52:20.879 --> 00:52:22.289
somewhere.

00:52:22.309 --> 00:52:25.019
I think that we've actually looked at some of the things

00:52:25.019 --> 00:52:28.319
that Moe was talking about. I don't know if we're good at

00:52:28.319 --> 00:52:30.929
knowing what's good there yet, but we're focusing there

00:52:30.929 --> 00:52:34.799
because of the constraints here that these guys know way

00:52:34.819 --> 00:52:40.730
better than I do. But yeah, I think every company should be

00:52:40.750 --> 00:52:44.529
thinking that way. But we're not seeing it in every company

00:52:44.549 --> 00:52:47.799
because sometimes we see it in a guy like Darren with a good

00:52:47.799 --> 00:52:50.049
idea and that's okay for the time being too.

00:52:50.049 --> 00:52:52.549
So, you have companies coming to you with medical devices

00:52:52.549 --> 00:52:56.009
that are saying, "We're doing $5,000-10,000, we're doing PMA

00:52:56.009 --> 00:52:58.210
in the U.S. and just kind of a standard..."

00:52:58.210 --> 00:52:59.210
Yeah. Yeah.

00:52:59.210 --> 00:53:01.000
Okay.

00:53:01.019 --> 00:53:06.079
And like you said earlier, there's still some things to be

00:53:06.099 --> 00:53:07.599
created in the good, old fashioned medical device industry

00:53:07.599 --> 00:53:08.599
too.

00:53:08.599 --> 00:53:09.599
Are you funding them?

00:53:09.599 --> 00:53:18.199
Yeah. We actually are.

00:53:18.219 --> 00:53:19.219
[Informal Talk]

00:53:19.219 --> 00:53:25.989
So, PMA devices go to other ventures and Darren will

00:53:26.009 --> 00:53:27.009
[inaudible]

00:53:27.009 --> 00:53:30.109
Darren, so both your strategy if you can tell us and your

00:53:30.129 --> 00:53:31.129
perspective.

00:53:31.129 --> 00:53:36.259
Yeah. So, I look at this no different than I look at the

00:53:36.279 --> 00:53:39.029
funding for the company because this is just kind of the

00:53:39.029 --> 00:53:41.529
next level of funding for the company and creativity is

00:53:41.529 --> 00:53:44.940
going to need to be involved for devices that are Class 3

00:53:44.960 --> 00:53:49.179
PMA devices because the FDA is not an easy place to get

00:53:49.199 --> 00:53:53.599
through nowadays. So, I'm really focusing the company

00:53:53.619 --> 00:53:57.819
outside of the United States and saying, "We need to be able

00:53:57.839 --> 00:54:01.869
to build to pay sustainable business in Europe and in other

00:54:01.889 --> 00:54:07.000
geographies. And we'll wait to see what's going to be

00:54:07.019 --> 00:54:11.299
happening with the FDA, but I'm not going to bet the entire

00:54:11.319 --> 00:54:14.690
company on that. It's just too uncertain."

00:54:14.710 --> 00:54:18.679
So, we like a lot of the opportunities outside. And so, we'

00:54:18.699 --> 00:54:21.759
re going to be gearing up for that kind of a structure. And

00:54:21.759 --> 00:54:29.059
on a personal level it kills me because there are a lot of

00:54:29.079 --> 00:54:32.539
patients I meet-I meet on planes, I go out and actively

00:54:32.559 --> 00:54:37.699
interview-and this is a therapy that I'm really excited to

00:54:37.719 --> 00:54:41.690
bring to patients here in our own country. And it's going to

00:54:41.710 --> 00:54:43.150
be a long time.

00:54:43.170 --> 00:54:45.750
So, are you talking about Europe really practically

00:54:45.769 --> 00:54:47.460
speaking? I mean, that's where you'll get traction?

00:54:47.460 --> 00:54:48.460
Yeah.

00:54:48.460 --> 00:54:49.460
Yeah.

00:54:49.460 --> 00:54:54.799
Europe, Australia. And these are just areas that

00:54:54.819 --> 00:55:00.739
unfortunately obesity is a global epidemic. So, we can go

00:55:00.759 --> 00:55:05.369
almost anywhere. But as Darren was saying, there are some

00:55:05.389 --> 00:55:08.519
very interesting countries like China and India where there'

00:55:08.539 --> 00:55:13.559
s more of a consumer pay event and that's a great start for

00:55:13.579 --> 00:55:14.579
us.

00:55:14.579 --> 00:55:20.449
What are you expecting to run into as a difficulty? What's

00:55:20.469 --> 00:55:22.710
your white hot risk with that strategy?

00:55:22.730 --> 00:55:25.869
People. People. So, it's just getting the right people

00:55:25.889 --> 00:55:30.159
aligned around the table that on one side are not myopically

00:55:30.179 --> 00:55:35.079
focused on the U.S and really not look at the other

00:55:35.099 --> 00:55:39.469
geographies not as owe U.S., but like as, "This can be the

00:55:39.489 --> 00:55:44.299
main entr?e". So, our biggest risk there is really going to

00:55:44.319 --> 00:55:48.579
be recruiting the right people to help us deliver on that

00:55:48.599 --> 00:55:49.599
strategy.

00:55:49.599 --> 00:55:53.190
But as far as, "Are the patients there?" "Is there money

00:55:53.210 --> 00:55:57.429
there?" That's not the issue for us.

00:55:57.449 --> 00:56:00.710
To what extent are you influenced? So, a common thing we

00:56:00.730 --> 00:56:03.960
hear is, "Gee, the big strategic's-the big companies-have

00:56:03.980 --> 00:56:07.529
all this offshore money that they don't want to repatriate,

00:56:07.549 --> 00:56:12.079
so great exit strategy to come up in Europe." Is that

00:56:12.099 --> 00:56:14.440
discussed around your board room?

00:56:14.460 --> 00:56:17.639
We're not too worried about repatriating money yet because

00:56:17.659 --> 00:56:28.819
we just want to make money. But I mean, let's be real.

00:56:28.839 --> 00:56:33.519
[inaudible] was a stunning success and we would love to

00:56:33.539 --> 00:56:37.369
follow that model of showing something that has tremendous

00:56:37.389 --> 00:56:41.650
clinical value in patients and then working with a large

00:56:41.670 --> 00:56:45.769
strategic to help make that a standard of care.

00:56:45.789 --> 00:56:50.349
And that was kind of what I learned at my time at Medtronic.

00:56:50.369 --> 00:56:55.119
There were a lot of other innovative things happening

00:56:55.139 --> 00:56:59.009
elsewhere in the medical world, but Medtronic was fantastic

00:56:59.029 --> 00:57:02.379
at distribution and truly making things a standard of care.

00:57:02.399 --> 00:57:05.679
And a big success for Hourglass is ensuring that our device

00:57:05.699 --> 00:57:09.049
gets to as many patients as possible. And so, we'll go

00:57:09.069 --> 00:57:11.869
through whatever channels are necessary to achieve that.

00:57:11.889 --> 00:57:14.920
Udai, your strategy?

00:57:14.940 --> 00:57:17.690
Oh yeah. Ours is a little different in a different way

00:57:17.690 --> 00:57:20.440
because we didn't have as much regulatory risk as the $5,

00:57:20.440 --> 00:57:23.440
000-10,000. We focused on the U.S. from the beginning. We

00:57:23.440 --> 00:57:25.940
talked a little bit about the reimbursement which is a

00:57:25.940 --> 00:57:28.690
moderate risk. But I think the other thing we wanted to

00:57:28.690 --> 00:57:31.690
think about is our device and our approach is very, very low

00:57:31.690 --> 00:57:34.190
cost. And we want to keep driving that cost lower.

00:57:34.190 --> 00:57:36.940
So, I think the biggest opportunities for us are in the

00:57:36.940 --> 00:57:40.549
[inaudible] in China eventually. But I think we can drive

00:57:40.569 --> 00:57:43.519
the cost of this product and our service even lower with

00:57:43.539 --> 00:57:46.649
volume. So, as we get volume in the U.S. that can help us

00:57:46.649 --> 00:57:50.969
drive manufacturing costs down. We can go to Canada. We can

00:57:50.989 --> 00:57:54.579
go to NHS as a system and say, "We can provide this device

00:57:54.599 --> 00:57:57.359
at far lower cost than anything you have right now" because

00:57:57.379 --> 00:58:02.609
we will have been able to deliver the cost reductions

00:58:02.629 --> 00:58:04.849
because of the volume we're seeing in the U.S.

00:58:04.849 --> 00:58:08.190
The other thing is from a strategic standpoint is at the end

00:58:08.210 --> 00:58:11.210
of the day I'm a Molecular Physiologist. So, I want to treat

00:58:11.210 --> 00:58:13.710
people who have arrhythmias because we can treat them. Big

00:58:13.710 --> 00:58:15.710
companies like Medtronics, St. Jude-St. Jude's an investor

00:58:15.710 --> 00:58:17.960
in our company along with Boston Scientific. They make

00:58:17.960 --> 00:58:19.460
products-very expensive products, but very efficacious

00:58:19.460 --> 00:58:21.960
products-for people who have certain arrhythmias. Emerging

00:58:21.980 --> 00:58:24.989
markets are a big focus for them now.

00:58:25.009 --> 00:58:27.839
So, at the end of the day it's very difficult to penetrate

00:58:27.839 --> 00:58:31.759
India with a $10,000-20,000 defibrillator and price it lower

00:58:31.779 --> 00:58:35.269
to get into the market or do what you have to. But to get a

00:58:35.269 --> 00:58:38.940
device that's $50-100 or whatever it might be broadly to

00:58:38.960 --> 00:58:43.049
diagnose people who may otherwise be left undiagnosed with

00:58:43.069 --> 00:58:45.699
vague symptoms, much easier value proposition to then

00:58:45.719 --> 00:58:48.469
convince some of them that, "Look, you have this big, huge

00:58:48.489 --> 00:58:51.179
pause where you don't have a heartbeat for six seconds.

00:58:51.179 --> 00:58:54.179
Maybe you do need a pacemaker." And with a device that they

00:58:54.179 --> 00:58:57.179
were able to buy like, "I'll do it", that's a selling point.

00:58:57.179 --> 00:59:00.909
So, our strategy is more from a strategic sales strategy in

00:59:00.929 --> 00:59:03.279
the use of the gold market cause I definitely think there's

00:59:03.279 --> 00:59:05.769
a bigger market for our product elsewhere. But right now the

00:59:05.769 --> 00:59:09.769
U.S. will prove it out for us to go all U.S. And again, the

00:59:09.769 --> 00:59:12.519
most important reason is we continue to export. I don't want

00:59:12.519 --> 00:59:14.529
to import. MedTech is a big...

00:59:14.549 --> 00:59:18.779
What's your protection in India and China?

00:59:18.799 --> 00:59:22.670
Well, from our PCT applications when we've started we've

00:59:22.690 --> 00:59:26.279
gone into Canada, UE, Japan, but not much in China because

00:59:26.299 --> 00:59:29.349
really it's still a wild west. So, I think we'll eventually

00:59:29.349 --> 00:59:34.409
get there, but we'll keep moving east to Europe until we get

00:59:34.429 --> 00:59:38.199
there. Hopefully some of the things we've seen on the

00:59:38.219 --> 00:59:40.639
Stanford India side, some of the more regulations and

00:59:40.659 --> 00:59:43.049
protects will be in place years from now.

00:59:43.069 --> 00:59:47.059
At the end of the day, finding more arrhythmias-if it floats

00:59:47.079 --> 00:59:49.969
everybody's boat in terms of devices-they have almost no

00:59:49.989 --> 00:59:53.349
penetration there. So, they're starting from scratch with an

00:59:53.369 --> 00:59:56.920
unpenetrated population of a billion plus. So, I don't know

00:59:56.940 --> 00:59:59.440
if that's going to be too much of a worry.

00:59:59.440 --> 01:00:02.440
Yeah. Moe, you're the fifth man on the panel. I'm going to

01:00:02.440 --> 01:00:05.440
ask you about your global strategy too if you would. But I

01:00:05.440 --> 01:00:08.379
also want to ask, anybody else in the audience who is

01:00:08.399 --> 01:00:14.719
primarily focusing on a developing nation strategy? Can you

01:00:14.739 --> 01:00:15.919
tell us about that quickly?

01:00:15.919 --> 01:00:16.919
[Informal talk]

01:00:15.919 --> 01:00:16.919
My name's Peter

01:00:16.919 --> 01:00:26.909
[inaudible] I'm a family physician. I developed a smart

01:00:26.929 --> 01:00:30.190
system for the neonatal space that actually Moe, kind of

01:00:30.210 --> 01:00:34.500
mirrors your product. We're going for low cost. The barriers

01:00:34.519 --> 01:00:40.059
here in the U.S. were just formidable. We're trying to push

01:00:40.079 --> 01:00:43.509
a device for risk reduction and lowering cost and we just

01:00:43.529 --> 01:00:46.349
don't find that VC's were open to that yet.

01:00:46.369 --> 01:00:53.489
So, India seems to be a place with open arms. And it's at

01:00:53.509 --> 01:01:02.289
low cost, very different market strategy than the U.S. Our

01:01:02.309 --> 01:01:05.319
cost point would be $20,000 here. We're going to try to

01:01:05.319 --> 01:01:09.210
bring it in to developing countries for $800 and using a

01:01:09.230 --> 01:01:13.609
three tier market where you get it out to the poor for free,

01:01:13.629 --> 01:01:15.259
and some of your

01:01:15.279 --> 01:01:17.849
[inaudible] care hospitals you have a premium price-a multi-

01:01:17.869 --> 01:01:20.210
tiered marketing strategy.

01:01:20.230 --> 01:01:27.440
And are you Geo funded? Is it for profit? What's your model?

01:01:27.460 --> 01:01:28.949
What's my...?

01:01:28.969 --> 01:01:29.969
The business model?

01:01:29.969 --> 01:01:34.819
We're still in very early startup. We just have our working

01:01:34.839 --> 01:01:38.909
prototype and we'll be pitching for different foundations

01:01:38.929 --> 01:01:39.929
here.

01:01:39.929 --> 01:01:40.929
Cool.

01:01:40.179 --> 01:01:41.309
So, towards July.

01:01:41.329 --> 01:01:47.259
Neat. Bon voyage. Other people who've done developing? Yeah,

01:01:47.279 --> 01:01:48.279
please.

01:01:48.279 --> 01:01:52.920
Hi, so I'm not with a small company, Verna Rodriguez with

01:01:52.940 --> 01:01:54.349
Boston Scientific. Hi Paul.

01:01:54.369 --> 01:01:55.369
I've heard of that.

01:01:55.369 --> 01:02:00.929
Yes. So, Boston Scientific has an emerging market

01:02:00.949 --> 01:02:04.539
initiative. So, from a large company perspective with all

01:02:04.559 --> 01:02:08.859
the changing healthcare and Boston Scientific historically

01:02:08.879 --> 01:02:13.309
being very myopically U.S. focused I think is waking up and

01:02:13.329 --> 01:02:16.339
saying, "Oh. We need to sort of change what we want to do in

01:02:16.339 --> 01:02:19.929
the world in terms of selling products and not be so U.S.

01:02:19.949 --> 01:02:21.049
focused."

01:02:21.069 --> 01:02:24.159
So, we're doing things like we already have some

01:02:24.179 --> 01:02:28.089
manufacturing in countries like Ireland and also Costa Rica.

01:02:28.109 --> 01:02:31.579
And we're expanding more in Costa Rica. But also, we have a

01:02:31.599 --> 01:02:34.969
small group of folks in China that's a Boston Scientific

01:02:34.989 --> 01:02:39.799
based company. And so, we're looking not only into those

01:02:39.819 --> 01:02:45.539
markets by actually manufacturing in those countries to

01:02:45.559 --> 01:02:50.049
reduce cost, but also to be able to have a presence in those

01:02:50.069 --> 01:02:54.509
markets so that we can invent and innovate devices that are

01:02:54.529 --> 01:02:58.469
for those markets, not with a U.S. focus. So, that's what

01:02:58.489 --> 01:03:03.099
Boston Scientific's doing. So, that's why I'm here.

01:03:03.119 --> 01:03:04.119
Yeah. Thank you.

01:03:04.119 --> 01:03:06.639
Stunning. I heard John

01:03:06.659 --> 01:03:09.269
[inaudible] remarks this morning about the shifts that they'

01:03:09.289 --> 01:03:14.279
re anticipating in five years time. It's just extraordinary.

01:03:14.299 --> 01:03:20.420
So Moe, tell us about your strategy.

01:03:20.440 --> 01:03:23.929
So, we mainly looked at diversifying reimbursement risk. And

01:03:23.949 --> 01:03:29.699
before going global one can think first creatively about

01:03:29.719 --> 01:03:34.619
alternative viewer spare. So, one system we like is the V8

01:03:34.639 --> 01:03:37.799
Medical System that dispenses about $78 million of

01:03:37.819 --> 01:03:41.699
healthcare spent each year. It's a relatively more

01:03:41.719 --> 01:03:45.469
straightforward process to get on the federal supply

01:03:45.489 --> 01:03:50.129
schedule and that's a way to basically diversify or mitigate

01:03:50.149 --> 01:03:53.179
a bit of the reimbursement risk, and something that we have

01:03:53.199 --> 01:03:58.179
implemented quite effectively.

01:03:58.199 --> 01:04:03.649
On the VA medical system with a product like ours, one may

01:04:03.669 --> 01:04:07.460
start thinking creatively about FEMA and all those disaster

01:04:07.480 --> 01:04:10.599
relief organizations. They're stalking those...

01:04:10.619 --> 01:04:12.619
Do you want to tell us about your product just quick

01:04:12.619 --> 01:04:13.619
because...

01:04:13.619 --> 01:04:17.149
Yeah. So, our product is basically a single use negative use

01:04:17.169 --> 01:04:22.789
therapy device for treatment of chronic wounds and it can

01:04:22.809 --> 01:04:26.409
help treat acute wounds as well. Once homeostasis has been

01:04:26.429 --> 01:04:31.980
achieved, negative pressure wound therapy which is the

01:04:32.000 --> 01:04:35.730
application of controlled suction to wounds through a formal

01:04:35.750 --> 01:04:41.419
gauze interface is the eighth largest VME category that

01:04:41.439 --> 01:04:45.699
Medicare spent. In the U.S. product sales are about $1.5

01:04:45.719 --> 01:04:52.980
billion and the category has grown over 600% in five years

01:04:53.000 --> 01:04:55.159
in terms of Medicare payment.

01:04:55.179 --> 01:04:58.059
And my frustrations even though we brought them we can

01:04:58.079 --> 01:05:00.949
probably just eliminate half of these cost just out of the

01:05:00.969 --> 01:05:07.509
box. That wasn't sufficient. But it's therapy that basically

01:05:07.529 --> 01:05:11.579
has brought application to a lot of soft issue defects. And

01:05:11.599 --> 01:05:15.929
our experience in the aftermath of the earthquake in Haiti

01:05:15.949 --> 01:05:21.029
where we sent one of our cofounders-a clinician-with a lot

01:05:21.049 --> 01:05:26.789
of products as part of an initiative with the University of

01:05:26.809 --> 01:05:32.049
Miami. We've seen that we were able to very effectively help

01:05:32.069 --> 01:05:35.190
folks in the aftermath of an earthquake especially when

01:05:35.210 --> 01:05:39.750
there are blackouts and all the electrical, capital

01:05:39.769 --> 01:05:43.669
equipment type devices just weren't able to be effective and

01:05:43.689 --> 01:05:47.659
they were treating folks in hospital tents.

01:05:47.679 --> 01:05:51.839
So, that kind of brought us the idea. "Well gee, we should

01:05:51.859 --> 01:05:58.419
be approaching FEMA and approaching DOD more to understand

01:05:58.439 --> 01:06:01.690
what are their criteria so that they would stock such a

01:06:01.710 --> 01:06:05.379
product as part of a relief or disaster effort." That could

01:06:05.399 --> 01:06:08.879
be one stocking or they could be probably our revenue

01:06:08.899 --> 01:06:10.710
projections for next year.

01:06:10.730 --> 01:06:14.159
Another one is of course the VA medical system. In terms of

01:06:14.179 --> 01:06:18.549
all U.S. we are implementing those very, very aggressively.

01:06:18.569 --> 01:06:27.049
So, one of the methods we use to stay capital efficient in

01:06:27.069 --> 01:06:30.559
our operations and be able to penetrate markets quickly is

01:06:30.579 --> 01:06:35.799
to really seek partners that would help us penetrate for

01:06:35.819 --> 01:06:39.109
example the European market. And that also ties a bit to our

01:06:39.129 --> 01:06:45.429
exit strategy. It's kind of, "Let's dance before we get

01:06:45.449 --> 01:06:50.119
married" kind of thing. So, there's some strategic rational

01:06:50.139 --> 01:06:56.789
with the choice of distributor we go with. And beyond that

01:06:56.809 --> 01:07:02.849
we've launched a regulatory process in Japan. We also flew a

01:07:02.869 --> 01:07:04.419
partner that invested in us.

01:07:04.439 --> 01:07:09.839
But I think all these OUS commercialization endeavors need

01:07:09.859 --> 01:07:14.519
to build on a bedrock of FDA clearance. For example, in

01:07:14.539 --> 01:07:16.309
Bedrock what's left after that is the

01:07:16.309 --> 01:07:19.500
[inaudible] and a fairly straightforward process to get

01:07:19.519 --> 01:07:22.019
clearance. If you don't have FDA clearance in the U.S. it's

01:07:22.019 --> 01:07:25.549
a different story. Same holds for Japan, although the

01:07:25.569 --> 01:07:26.569
process is more...

01:07:26.569 --> 01:07:27.750
Your FDA pathway was?

01:07:27.769 --> 01:07:32.339
Our FDA pathway was not a $5,000-10,000. It was a noble

01:07:32.359 --> 01:07:36.019
process, but took 18 months, something that I thought would

01:07:36.039 --> 01:07:37.039
take less than that.

01:07:37.039 --> 01:07:38.039
[Overlapping]

01:07:38.039 --> 01:07:40.779
This may be an irritating question, but it seems to me you

01:07:40.799 --> 01:07:46.179
must be worried about people reengineering your device...

01:07:46.199 --> 01:07:47.199
Yeah.

01:07:47.199 --> 01:07:49.239
...especially in Asia. So, do you have a strategy about

01:07:49.239 --> 01:07:50.239
that?

01:07:50.239 --> 01:07:54.500
Yeah. Yeah. Our strategy may sound funny to you guys. It's

01:07:54.519 --> 01:07:57.769
get a partner who's dad is really senior in the Communist

01:07:57.789 --> 01:08:03.409
party so nobody will copy your device. So, what we're

01:08:03.429 --> 01:08:04.429
working on

01:08:04.429 --> 01:08:06.429
[Overlapping] identify guys who are really entwined in the

01:08:06.429 --> 01:08:14.779
political apparatus and those are usually the joint ventures

01:08:14.799 --> 01:08:18.169
and the operations that end up with their IP rights

01:08:18.189 --> 01:08:20.639
protected. So, that's really our strategy.

01:08:20.659 --> 01:08:24.359
By the way, we have submitted national phase applications

01:08:24.380 --> 01:08:27.979
hosting China and Japan. But I'm just looking at the date.

01:08:28.000 --> 01:08:34.539
Until early this year there hasn't been a single foreign

01:08:34.559 --> 01:08:40.159
company that has won a trial case in Japan in respective of

01:08:40.179 --> 01:08:42.220
the rights and the contracts that were signed. So, we're

01:08:42.220 --> 01:08:44.529
doing that but...

01:08:44.550 --> 01:08:47.109
But you haven't seen devices emerging yet that are

01:08:47.130 --> 01:08:48.130
competitive.

01:08:48.130 --> 01:08:51.709
We haven't seen devices emerging yet that are competitive.

01:08:51.729 --> 01:08:55.479
We have seen interesting ideas coming from academia and from

01:08:55.500 --> 01:08:56.500
students working in

01:08:56.500 --> 01:08:58.889
[inaudible] That's our area of concern.

01:08:58.909 --> 01:08:59.909
Good. Thanks.

01:08:59.909 --> 01:09:00.909
Sure.

01:09:00.909 --> 01:09:08.279
So Moe, you have a clear developing world strategy and that

01:09:08.300 --> 01:09:17.469
makes great sense. Darren, for the high end medical devices

01:09:17.489 --> 01:09:20.489
is there a developing world strategy? Is that even on your

01:09:20.510 --> 01:09:22.679
radar screen?

01:09:22.699 --> 01:09:25.920
To be honest it really isn't as much on our radar screen.

01:09:25.920 --> 01:09:29.469
Well, I guess I should ask which geographies you're

01:09:29.489 --> 01:09:30.719
referring to?

01:09:30.739 --> 01:09:33.149
Just riff on it, whatever you want to say.

01:09:33.170 --> 01:09:40.209
So, it's hard to even think about whether Brazil, and China,

01:09:40.229 --> 01:09:42.989
and India are developing or whether they're way ahead of the

01:09:42.989 --> 01:09:46.659
United States at this point. So, depending how you look at

01:09:46.679 --> 01:09:47.679
it...

01:09:47.679 --> 01:09:48.679
Right.

01:09:47.929 --> 01:09:48.929
But...

01:09:48.279 --> 01:09:51.409
Advanced developing. That's it, yeah.

01:09:51.429 --> 01:09:55.949
But no, I mean I was just talking to some surgeons in Brazil

01:09:55.970 --> 01:10:01.339
this past weekend at a large medical conference and I look

01:10:01.359 --> 01:10:03.849
at the infrastructure they have down there. It's really

01:10:03.849 --> 01:10:07.089
exciting! Our technology would dovetail beautifully into

01:10:07.109 --> 01:10:10.999
that because even at the heart of it what we're doing is we'

01:10:11.019 --> 01:10:15.459
re taking a very invasive technology and making it basically

01:10:15.479 --> 01:10:18.649
a conscious evasion outpatient procedure. It's moving from

01:10:18.670 --> 01:10:23.649
expensive OR to a much less expensive endoscopy suite. And

01:10:23.670 --> 01:10:28.269
that's a model that could work very well in regions where

01:10:28.289 --> 01:10:32.709
capital costs could be a huge barrier to offer procedures

01:10:32.729 --> 01:10:33.879
for patients.

01:10:33.899 --> 01:10:42.709
So, we definitely see there's plenty of opportunity there.

01:10:42.729 --> 01:10:45.949
It's incredible actually how much the procedure volumes were

01:10:45.970 --> 01:10:50.049
growing in Brazil. So, that's a market we're going to be

01:10:50.069 --> 01:10:51.069
monitoring very closely.

01:10:51.069 --> 01:10:55.099
So, we're going to get in just a minute to asking you guys

01:10:55.119 --> 01:10:57.649
to look around the corner. And again, I'd welcome any

01:10:57.649 --> 01:11:01.839
questions about that from the audience. But sort of to prime

01:11:01.859 --> 01:11:05.219
the pump for that, one thing that I'm hearing a lot anyway

01:11:05.239 --> 01:11:09.519
from young innovators, young companies is that really the

01:11:09.539 --> 01:11:12.979
name of the game now is to figure out what the strategics

01:11:13.000 --> 01:11:19.699
want to do right away, if possible have that discussion and

01:11:19.720 --> 01:11:25.649
get funding at a seed stage or Series A. What do you see or

01:11:25.670 --> 01:11:28.389
what do you think about that? Maybe I'll start with Darren

01:11:28.389 --> 01:11:29.899
[inaudible] again on that.

01:11:29.920 --> 01:11:30.920
On what specifically?

01:11:30.920 --> 01:11:36.999
On the idea being that you need to know because it's so

01:11:37.019 --> 01:11:42.069
tough now to go through a series-A...B...C...D that you

01:11:42.090 --> 01:11:46.839
really need a partnership worked out with a strategic early

01:11:46.859 --> 01:11:50.589
on and the best thing to do is to really start right away

01:11:50.609 --> 01:11:53.469
with that kind of partnership discussion.

01:11:53.489 --> 01:11:56.569
I don't know if that's the best thing to do to start that

01:11:56.569 --> 01:12:01.529
way actually. I think the places where we've succeeded in

01:12:01.550 --> 01:12:04.189
dealing with bigger companies is usually to have a

01:12:04.210 --> 01:12:07.899
technology that scares them a little bit, that takes their

01:12:07.920 --> 01:12:12.889
mind share of their key physicians or you can go to Europe

01:12:12.909 --> 01:12:18.999
let's say and have their highest user physician start to use

01:12:19.019 --> 01:12:22.939
your product and then tell his friends, then get them to

01:12:22.960 --> 01:12:25.899
start using the product, and then all the sudden they really

01:12:25.920 --> 01:12:29.859
want to be your partner. Before then it hasn't really

01:12:29.880 --> 01:12:31.569
happened as much for us.

01:12:31.590 --> 01:12:34.239
Moe, in your world?

01:12:34.260 --> 01:12:37.159
I don't think I'm doing anything that big MedTech would be

01:12:37.159 --> 01:12:39.409
interested in because I'm so different. I'm actually getting

01:12:39.409 --> 01:12:43.649
more interest from the strategic's like Intel and Cisco.

01:12:43.670 --> 01:12:44.670
Sure.

01:12:44.670 --> 01:12:45.670
How about you, Darren?

01:12:45.670 --> 01:12:49.179
I mean, this has been our model. We were in a garage when

01:12:49.199 --> 01:12:52.769
Johnson &amp; Johnson invested in us. I don't know if they knew

01:12:52.789 --> 01:12:57.969
we were in a garage at the time. Looking at the capital

01:12:57.989 --> 01:13:03.719
requirements for a Class 3 device and I wanted to make sure

01:13:03.739 --> 01:13:07.859
that there would be a tremendous amount of alignment. And I

01:13:07.880 --> 01:13:10.789
know that there was a tremendous amount of controversy when

01:13:10.809 --> 01:13:14.319
I talked to advisors and mentors about, "Do you bring a

01:13:14.340 --> 01:13:17.359
strategic in early?" "What are the pros and cons there?" "Do

01:13:17.380 --> 01:13:21.119
they put handcuffs on you?" And it's all how you structure a

01:13:21.139 --> 01:13:24.329
deal. You can make it so that it works great for the

01:13:24.349 --> 01:13:25.349
company.

01:13:25.349 --> 01:13:28.869
And Johnson &amp; Johnson has been a tremendous partner and has

01:13:28.889 --> 01:13:33.249
been extremely helpful. So, if I were to do it all over

01:13:33.269 --> 01:13:36.639
again I would certainly jump into bed early with a

01:13:36.659 --> 01:13:42.049
strategic. I think they provide profound insights on what

01:13:42.069 --> 01:13:43.359
the market's doing right now.

01:13:43.359 --> 01:13:45.859
Well, what does that look like? What is your actual

01:13:45.859 --> 01:13:49.319
interface with the company? I mean, what kinds of people are

01:13:49.340 --> 01:13:55.889
helping you?

01:13:55.909 --> 01:13:59.069
Obviously Johnson &amp; Johnson's a very large company. Ethicon

01:13:59.090 --> 01:14:03.739
Endo surgery is the segment that focuses on bariatric

01:14:03.760 --> 01:14:09.409
surgery as well as all surgical type devices and it gives us

01:14:09.429 --> 01:14:13.579
the ability to interface with experts from reimbursement-

01:14:13.599 --> 01:14:18.099
clinical, regulatory, marketing. It really brings tremendous

01:14:18.119 --> 01:14:21.439
amounts of resources to bear that frankly we could never

01:14:21.460 --> 01:14:25.249
afford as a startup company.

01:14:25.269 --> 01:14:27.349
Udai, you have a relationship with St. Jude?

01:14:27.369 --> 01:14:30.619
Yeah. So, St. Jude invested in us in our "B" round after we

01:14:30.619 --> 01:14:33.349
had clearance and were really starting to market our

01:14:33.369 --> 01:14:37.469
product. And I think that's more typical. They want to see a

01:14:37.489 --> 01:14:40.239
little bit later stage where there's going to perhaps be an

01:14:40.239 --> 01:14:42.989
impact that some of the regulatory or other risks have been

01:14:42.989 --> 01:14:45.739
taken off the table. While I think that all the strategic's

01:14:45.739 --> 01:14:48.239
are thinking they'd like to invest earlier I think the

01:14:48.239 --> 01:14:50.989
reality is you might be the exception that it doesn't really

01:14:50.989 --> 01:14:53.389
happen because it's still too different from their DNA at

01:14:53.389 --> 01:14:54.389
the moment.

01:14:54.389 --> 01:14:57.380
So, I don't know how often that's happened. The other thing

01:14:57.380 --> 01:15:00.880
I would say is when you do make a deal with a strategic, if

01:15:00.880 --> 01:15:03.569
you have multiple potential strategic exit partners there's

01:15:03.590 --> 01:15:07.899
a plus and minus of having one of those as your partner. So,

01:15:07.920 --> 01:15:11.939
our deal leaves the backend open. It's just that they're an

01:15:11.960 --> 01:15:15.089
investor and we have a cool marketing relationship. So, they

01:15:15.109 --> 01:15:18.039
help market our products, but that's where it ends.

01:15:18.059 --> 01:15:21.239
So, I do think it's very important to make sure that when

01:15:21.239 --> 01:15:24.139
you structure those deals if possible you think about the

01:15:24.159 --> 01:15:26.699
pros and cons, and what that means downstream because if

01:15:26.719 --> 01:15:28.429
they're getting everything they want from you and you're

01:15:28.429 --> 01:15:31.999
locked into them, that could be a tough situation for you

01:15:32.019 --> 01:15:34.289
whereas if you're not, that could be actually to your

01:15:34.289 --> 01:15:35.289
advantage.

01:15:35.289 --> 01:15:38.539
Yes. So, we have seen some deals with people trying to lock

01:15:38.559 --> 01:15:41.569
up our companies. "We'll invest early, but if you're ever

01:15:41.590 --> 01:15:45.739
anything we'll own you" and that's a bad position to be in.

01:15:45.760 --> 01:15:47.779
for sure.

01:15:47.800 --> 01:15:51.489
Anybody with experience in the audience? Are you in

01:15:51.510 --> 01:15:55.519
companies that have a close strategic partner? Anybody here?

01:15:55.539 --> 01:15:59.559
A few of the venture people are seeing these I'm sure at

01:15:59.579 --> 01:16:03.109
this point. But Minnow, any comment about this from your

01:16:03.130 --> 01:16:07.029
standpoint? So, if you don't know Minnow, Minnow's one of

01:16:07.050 --> 01:16:11.389
the very experienced serial CEO's in MedTech in the valley.

01:16:11.409 --> 01:16:16.649
Thanks Paul. I think everything that is not general rules

01:16:16.670 --> 01:16:20.109
and as we can see on the panel here the gentleman has a

01:16:20.130 --> 01:16:23.739
terrific relationship with J &amp; J. I'm sure he put together a

01:16:23.760 --> 01:16:27.010
deal that he can get out of it and actually would be more

01:16:27.010 --> 01:16:30.559
attractive to potential other buyers knowing that J &amp; J has

01:16:30.579 --> 01:16:32.509
interest in his deal.

01:16:32.529 --> 01:16:34.869
That's right.

01:16:34.889 --> 01:16:41.029
And I think that we need to be very open to learn and find

01:16:41.050 --> 01:16:43.909
out where the opportunities are whether we're doing a global

01:16:43.929 --> 01:16:48.469
deal or maybe the best opportunities in the U.S. What are

01:16:48.489 --> 01:16:50.989
you trying to accomplish? Are you trying to prove the

01:16:50.989 --> 01:16:55.589
product works? How can you prove it? Maybe you don't have to

01:16:55.609 --> 01:17:00.429
go through FDA approval. Maybe you can do basically a FIM in

01:17:00.449 --> 01:17:04.869
the U.S. and that will trigger a nice deal for you.

01:17:04.889 --> 01:17:08.319
So, I would recommend that basically the market and the

01:17:08.340 --> 01:17:12.769
needs are continuously changing. It's like a pendulum. We're

01:17:12.789 --> 01:17:16.549
talking about healthcare reform where my big success was in

01:17:16.569 --> 01:17:21.469
1995 when Hillary Clinton tried to introduce healthcare

01:17:21.489 --> 01:17:25.739
reform and cost was everything that everybody talked about.

01:17:25.760 --> 01:17:28.599
And I had a product that basically I didn't even know what

01:17:28.599 --> 01:17:34.779
it was good for. And then I did a clinical study to prove

01:17:34.800 --> 01:17:39.329
its clinical value and I found out that it can cut

01:17:39.349 --> 01:17:43.669
significantly the number of angioplasties in the U.S. It

01:17:43.689 --> 01:17:47.679
happened to be at that time health care reform cost savings

01:17:47.699 --> 01:17:51.999
capitation was the big buzz. The cardiologist obviously

01:17:52.019 --> 01:17:55.429
didn't like it. Those are my customers. But the financial

01:17:55.449 --> 01:17:58.889
community loved it and I was able to take the company

01:17:58.909 --> 01:18:03.439
public. But then after a year and a half or two healthcare

01:18:03.460 --> 01:18:21.349
reform didn't work. Then I rushed to sell it.

01:18:21.369 --> 01:18:23.119
Alright. So, let's bring this around. Alright

01:18:23.119 --> 01:18:24.119
[Informal talk]

01:18:23.119 --> 01:18:24.519
My name is

01:18:24.539 --> 01:18:27.039
[inaudible] I have a long history with Guidant and a few

01:18:27.039 --> 01:18:31.129
other companies. I'm a venture capitalist and I'm learning a

01:18:31.149 --> 01:18:35.169
lot. I'm really happy to hear that people are thinking now

01:18:35.189 --> 01:18:40.929
about countries like Germany, and Holland which is the

01:18:40.949 --> 01:18:42.169
Netherlands by the way.

01:18:42.189 --> 01:18:46.939
And so, I have a couple of things that I really want to

01:18:46.960 --> 01:18:49.710
point out. It's nothing. There's a lot of advice that you

01:18:49.710 --> 01:18:52.710
said you think we should make up our own minds about things.

01:18:52.710 --> 01:18:57.379
First, about dealing with large companies I think it's in

01:18:57.399 --> 01:19:01.989
your best interest as a CEO to start early having

01:19:02.010 --> 01:19:08.819
conversations with strategic's-and there only about 10-

01:19:08.840 --> 01:19:11.599
because you're going to learn a lot. You're going to learn

01:19:11.599 --> 01:19:14.519
how they think and when the moment is right you can kind of

01:19:14.519 --> 01:19:15.519
step in.

01:19:15.519 --> 01:19:20.669
You're right. There are lots of ways of structuring this.

01:19:20.689 --> 01:19:25.269
And so I don't think it hurts to speak with them and even to

01:19:25.289 --> 01:19:29.929
get an investment because all of the large companies have

01:19:29.949 --> 01:19:34.399
now stepped in where VC's have now stepped out. And so, they

01:19:34.420 --> 01:19:40.039
are actually very attractive potential investors because

01:19:40.059 --> 01:19:45.579
they are taking more risks because in the P &amp; L it doesn't

01:19:45.599 --> 01:19:48.939
mean a hell of a lot. And particularly where they are

01:19:48.960 --> 01:19:52.589
talking about the white spaces I think it can be really

01:19:52.609 --> 01:19:56.519
interesting. But also, large companies are doing two things.

01:19:56.539 --> 01:20:01.179
They want to feed the distribution system or they want to

01:20:01.199 --> 01:20:03.949
fill the white spaces. And so, you've got to kind of

01:20:03.949 --> 01:20:06.389
understand where you're going to fit.

01:20:06.409 --> 01:20:08.889
And while I have the microphone Paul...

01:20:08.909 --> 01:20:09.909
Please.

01:20:09.909 --> 01:20:19.169
Europe doesn't exist in healthcare. You should really

01:20:19.189 --> 01:20:21.939
understand that very well. Every country you can only get a

01:20:21.939 --> 01:20:25.189
CE mark and that's just about it. And the problem is most of

01:20:25.189 --> 01:20:28.589
your boards don't understand that either and there's a lot

01:20:28.609 --> 01:20:34.719
of talk about this. But once you have a CE mark every

01:20:34.739 --> 01:20:38.029
country will have more clinical data and will particularly

01:20:38.050 --> 01:20:42.499
want what is called now health technology assessments. So,

01:20:42.519 --> 01:20:47.779
they will want to establish in their particular country if

01:20:47.800 --> 01:20:49.119
this is of value.

01:20:49.139 --> 01:20:54.809
In Europe, we have the system as thought about value and in

01:20:54.829 --> 01:20:58.549
the cost of the system doctors get educated in thinking

01:20:58.569 --> 01:21:03.899
about the cost when they prescribe a therapy.

01:21:03.920 --> 01:21:09.359
So, every country is different on how they reimburse the

01:21:09.380 --> 01:21:11.659
system. Some countries don't really have a reimbursement

01:21:11.679 --> 01:21:18.359
system, but they have hospital budgets. So, my point is you

01:21:18.380 --> 01:21:24.639
want to really focus on one of two countries and go deep in

01:21:24.659 --> 01:21:27.219
a few centers because you're going to waste a tremendous

01:21:27.219 --> 01:21:29.969
amount of money. And you have to understand why you're going

01:21:29.969 --> 01:21:34.479
outside of those countries. There's two reasons basically

01:21:34.500 --> 01:21:38.609
why you want to do it is if you have a BMA you want to

01:21:38.630 --> 01:21:44.939
understand better how your therapy works and adjust it as

01:21:44.960 --> 01:21:48.849
you go. So, once you file for an IND then you know you have

01:21:48.869 --> 01:21:51.669
a very high probability that you're actually going to be

01:21:51.689 --> 01:21:54.899
successful and you're going to get BMA approval.

01:21:54.920 --> 01:21:56.029
And I think that's what

01:21:56.029 --> 01:21:57.779
[inaudible] did. They established very well the therapy.

01:21:57.779 --> 01:22:04.189
Now, we understand this therapy actually works and before

01:22:04.210 --> 01:22:10.229
the company has actually an IND just like

01:22:10.250 --> 01:22:13.179
[inaudible] they monetize their investments.

01:22:13.199 --> 01:22:16.889
[inaudible] existed for more than 10 years and

01:22:16.909 --> 01:22:19.999
[inaudible] was only 10 years. Back to your point, if it's

01:22:20.019 --> 01:22:23.909
big therapy, it doesn't have to be IT.

01:22:23.929 --> 01:22:28.539
So, the second thing why you would want to go there is

01:22:28.559 --> 01:22:35.509
basically to prove particularly to the large companies is

01:22:35.529 --> 01:22:41.979
that you actually have adaption. People meeting doctors will

01:22:42.000 --> 01:22:44.999
prescribe you therapy and hospitals will actually pay for

01:22:45.019 --> 01:22:48.319
it. And so, because then they think, "Alright. This is

01:22:48.340 --> 01:22:54.259
something very worthwhile, I can integrate this in my

01:22:54.279 --> 01:22:55.769
distribution system." Sorry.

01:22:55.789 --> 01:23:00.679
Well, thank you. It's great to have experts like this. And

01:23:00.699 --> 01:23:04.409
speaking of that, Anne we'll have to keep this short because

01:23:04.429 --> 01:23:05.929
we need to wrap, but please.

01:23:05.929 --> 01:23:09.119
Yeah. From an Asian perspective, first I have to agree with

01:23:09.139 --> 01:23:11.689
[inaudible] as a good Belgium. Europe is a whole bunch of

01:23:11.689 --> 01:23:13.469
different countries that happen to be on the same continent.

01:23:13.469 --> 01:23:14.469
Right.

01:23:14.469 --> 01:23:17.929
But from an Asian point of view anything we see that's a

01:23:17.949 --> 01:23:22.159
point of that, we punt except we've made exceptions and the

01:23:22.179 --> 01:23:25.769
one we invested in is company that's a small amount of

01:23:25.789 --> 01:23:26.969
capital. The

01:23:26.989 --> 01:23:29.149
[inaudible] product and market adoption and we usually do

01:23:29.149 --> 01:23:30.439
that by going to Europe.

01:23:30.460 --> 01:23:32.949
When we talk to the big corporation what they're telling us

01:23:32.949 --> 01:23:36.919
to say, "We want to redo the PMA and the ID our way anyway

01:23:36.939 --> 01:23:39.800
because we don't think you know how to do quality control.

01:23:39.800 --> 01:23:42.800
So, don't spend a lot of money there. Just show me this

01:23:42.800 --> 01:23:45.800
market adaption with a few key centers and that will buy you

01:23:45.800 --> 01:23:47.800
out." And these other deals are being funded.

01:23:47.800 --> 01:23:50.550
The other comment I wanted to make was early on the global

01:23:50.550 --> 01:23:51.550
we have a

01:23:51.550 --> 01:23:53.550
[inaudible] company. These companies are in India. They have

01:23:53.550 --> 01:23:56.300
great traction and they've got government to pass a law to

01:23:56.300 --> 01:23:59.300
say, "You have to do it". The problem is getting them funded

01:23:59.300 --> 01:24:02.300
for B &amp; C because venture capitalists in the U.S. and they'

01:24:02.300 --> 01:24:04.300
re still not comfortable even investing in American

01:24:04.300 --> 01:24:07.579
corporation where the market is overseas. So, we have a

01:24:07.599 --> 01:24:08.599
problem scaling these models.

01:24:08.599 --> 01:24:13.929
Yeah, good points. Thanks. And this does bring us to final

01:24:13.949 --> 01:24:17.469
thoughts. And we have less than a minute each, but let me

01:24:17.489 --> 01:24:24.769
ask you to think about suppose that you all got bought out

01:24:24.789 --> 01:24:29.579
tomorrow at tremendous success. What's your next thing? What

01:24:29.599 --> 01:24:32.689
direction and what piece of advice do you have? And we'll

01:24:32.710 --> 01:24:35.889
just go down the line, a succinct piece of advice, Udai?

01:24:35.909 --> 01:24:43.509
Well, that would be I think I'd take a vacation and I think

01:24:43.529 --> 01:24:48.379
what I would do is I think is the only thing that's valuable

01:24:48.399 --> 01:24:52.609
in life is time. Money can only buy time. I have two little

01:24:52.630 --> 01:24:55.380
kids. I spend a lot of time with them, I spend more time

01:24:55.380 --> 01:24:56.380
with them.

01:24:56.380 --> 01:24:57.380
Okay. Darren?

01:24:57.380 --> 01:25:01.489
Well, my first little one's on the way. So, I'm sure I'll

01:25:01.510 --> 01:25:07.129
learn that lesson very quickly. What has been really excited

01:25:07.149 --> 01:25:10.529
about Hourglass that I want to continue is the focus on

01:25:10.550 --> 01:25:16.189
consumers-patients as consumers. And I think I may shift

01:25:16.210 --> 01:25:21.189
more towards devices that patients can actually just buy and

01:25:21.210 --> 01:25:25.109
pay for themselves and that when they really see the value,

01:25:25.130 --> 01:25:29.049
then I think it's a great medical device. So, I'm looking

01:25:29.069 --> 01:25:30.989
for more consumer centric technology.

01:25:31.010 --> 01:25:35.069
Great. I'm a big believer when there's big regulatory change

01:25:35.090 --> 01:25:38.269
there's big opportunities at the tail end of that So, I

01:25:38.289 --> 01:25:41.289
would keep a close eye on health reform and just think of,

01:25:41.289 --> 01:25:43.289
again the fundamental statistics. Healthcare costs are going

01:25:43.289 --> 01:25:45.539
up. We're getting more elderly people in the population.

01:25:45.539 --> 01:25:48.289
They have more chronic disease which drives more cost and we

01:25:48.289 --> 01:25:50.579
now have less physicians because Stanford kids are retiring

01:25:50.599 --> 01:25:51.750
and going to business school.

01:25:51.750 --> 01:25:55.369
So, technology and solutions can help solve or mitigate some

01:25:55.389 --> 01:25:58.139
of those problems. So, it's a good place to think through.

01:25:58.139 --> 01:26:01.479
Yeah. I would just start by saying I think it's a really

01:26:01.500 --> 01:26:04.250
exciting time to be doing what we're doing. I think that

01:26:04.250 --> 01:26:07.500
there's a lot of change happening. And I think a lot of good

01:26:07.500 --> 01:26:10.750
will come out of it, and a lot of opportunity will come out

01:26:10.750 --> 01:26:14.249
of it. I like the fact that convergence of other industries

01:26:14.269 --> 01:26:16.639
outside of healthcare finally getting into healthcare. That'

01:26:16.659 --> 01:26:21.069
s happened in Finance and not so much in Education but it's

01:26:21.090 --> 01:26:24.469
on its way there too. So, I would focus on how mobile

01:26:24.489 --> 01:26:29.619
devices-internet, IT, cloud computing-can actually get into

01:26:29.639 --> 01:26:32.959
healthcare and make meaningful change to healthcare. So, I'm

01:26:32.979 --> 01:26:33.979
optimistic.

01:26:33.979 --> 01:26:37.729
Well, I'm sure the panelists will stick around for a little

01:26:37.750 --> 01:26:41.000
bit after we wind up. This really brings us to the end of

01:26:41.000 --> 01:26:45.949
the summit. I see some of the organizers in the audience.

01:26:45.969 --> 01:26:49.779
Congratulations on a terrific day. Thank you and

01:26:49.800 --> 01:26:50.800
[inaudible]

